\subsection{Problem statement}

We consider the setting where an offline dataset consisting of safe and unsafe trajectories is available. 
% From this dataset, we learn a control-affine dynamics model and a nominal controller using offline learning methods. 
Our objective is to train a safety filter that prevents the system from reaching unsafe states. Consequently, it must also prevent the system from reaching OOD ones at which it might not be reliable enough to determines safe from unsafe states and actions. The safety filter should not be conservative beyond what is necessary to maintain safety, i.e., as minimally affecting nominal control as possible. 
% train a neural control barrier function also from offline data, that can be deployed as a safety filter to monitor and modify the nominal controller.
% The learned CBF should ensure that, during deployment, the system remains within the set of safe and in-distribution states by minimally intervening on the nominal control actions through the solution of a quadratic program (QP), thereby maintaining both safety and reliable task performance under distributional shift.

\section{Method}
% This section discusses how we learn the CCBF, the dynamics, and the latent representation of the vision-based environment.
%To use the CBF-QP, we must learn the CBF and the policy using offline data.
Our aim is to design a CBF-QP policy from offline data. If the reference policy or the dynamics are not available, we train corresponding models using the same data. Moreover, in settings with high-dimensional observations, we train representation models and consider the latent space to be the state space based on which we train the dynamics model and the neural CBF.
