% \begin{equation}
% \frac{1}{T_{1/2}^{0\nu\beta\beta}}=m_{\beta\beta}^2\times({\rm nuclear~matrix~element})\times({\rm phase~space})
% \label{eq:0nubbrate}
% \end{equation}
% where $T_{1/2}^{0\nu\beta\beta}$ is the half life for $0\nu\beta\beta$ decay and
% \begin{equation}
% m_{\beta\beta}^2=\bigg|\sum_{i=1,2,3}U_{ei}^2m_i\bigg|^2.
% \label{eq:mbetabeta}
% \end{equation}
% Here $m_{1,2,3}$ are the masses of the light neutrinos and $U_{ei}$ are elements of the first row of the Pontecorvo–Maki–Nakagawa–Sakata matrix relating neutrino flavour eigenstates to mass eigenstates. 

Assuming that exchange of the light neutrinos dominates the matrix element, measuring the $0\nu\beta\beta$ rate of a particular nucleus would yield a direct measurement of the light neutrino masses in the event that they are Majorana\new{:}, %\new{i.e., yield} through the relationship
%\begin{equation}
%\frac{1}{T_{1/2}^{0\nu\beta\beta}}=m_{\beta\beta}^2\times({\rm nuclear~matrix~element})\times({\rm phase~space})
%\label{eq:0nubbrate}
%\end{equation}
%where $T_{1/2}^{0\nu\beta\beta}$ is the half life for $0\nu\beta\beta$ decay and
\begin{equation}
m_{\beta\beta}^2=\bigg|\sum_{i=1,2,3}U_{ei}^2m_i\bigg|^2.
\label{eq:mbetabeta}
\end{equation}
Here, $m_{1,2,3}$ are the masses of the light neutrinos and $U_{ei}$ are elements of the first row of the Pontecorvo–Maki–Nakagawa–Sakata matrix relating neutrino flavour eigenstates to mass eigenstates. This is discussed in the following section.


