\section{The Rotating Wave Approximation}\label{sect: RWA}



\begin{boxA}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The rotating-wave approximation (RWA) is generally needed to convert the Redfield equation \eqref{eq:red1} into the Lindblad equation \eqref{eq: lindblad} and thus to ensure that positivity of $\rho_S(t)$ is preserved.
        \item One has to look at the frequencies $\Omega$ of the \emph{transitions} that the bath induces in the system and their differences $\Delta\Omega$, see \fig~\ref{fig:rwa}. RWA is valid if the system contains only perfectly degenerate transitions $\Delta\Omega=0$ or far-detuned transitions $\Delta\Omega\tau_R\gg1$.
        \item Nearly degenerate transitions $0<\Delta\Omega\tau_R\lesssim1$ require treatment beyond the Lindblad equation.
    \end{itemize}
\end{boxA}


