The distribution $q^*$ that optimizes~\eqref{eq:me0} lies in the %on a manifold of probability distributions forming 
exponential family,
\begin{align}
    q_{\params}(\traj) = \tilde p(\traj) e^{{\params}^\top \gobs(\traj)-\ln \langle e^{{\params}^\top \gobs}\rangle_{\tilde{p}}}.\label{eq:expfam}
\end{align}
% Using this, 
We can interpret the optimal $\params^*$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:dual} as the optimal parameters in the maximum likelihood (ML) problem $\params^* = \argmin_{\params} D(p\Vert q_{\params})$, since $q_{\params^*}=q^*$ satisfies the constraints in \eqref{eq:me0} and achieves the optimum objective value (see \emph{End Matter}).  
This interpretation also leads to the information-geometric Pythagorean theorem~\cite{amari2001information}, which decomposes EP into two nonnegative KL terms:
\begin{align}
   \underbrace{ D(p\Vert \tilde{p}) }_{\ep}=& \underbrace{D(q^* \Vert \tilde p)}_{\epG} +
     \underbrace{D(p\Vert q^*)}_{\epNotG}.
     \label{eq:ep-decomposition}
\end{align}

The term $\epNotG:=\ep - \epG=D(p\Vert q^*)$ quantifies the EP not captured by $\epG$. It can be expressed in terms of a ML problem, $\epNotG=\min_{\params \in \mathbb{R}^d}D( p\Vert q_\params)$. To interpret this expression, we define a larger exponential family $\omega_{f}(\traj):=\tilde p(\traj)e^{f(\traj)}/\langle e^{f}\rangle_{\tilde p}$ that contains the forward trajectory distribution $p(\traj)=\omega_{\sigma}(\traj)$. The ML problem can be written as
\begin{align}
\epNotG=\min_{\params \in \mathbb{R}^d} D( \omega_{\sigma}\Vert \omega_{\params^\top \gobs}).
\label{eq:ml}
\end{align}
From this expression, it is clear that $\epNotG=0$ (and $\epG=\ep$) if and only if trajectory EP is a linear combination of the trajectory observables, i.e., $\sigma(\traj) = \params^{*\top} \gobs(\traj)$ for all $\traj$. When $\epNotG>0$, $\params^{*\top} \gobs(\traj)$ provides the best possible approximation of $\sigma(\traj)$ in the span of $\gobs$.

Eq.~\eqref{eq:expfam} implies that the conditional distribution of the optimal $q^*$ obeys
$q^*_{\Trajvar  \vert \Gvar}(\traj\vert \gobs')=\tilde p_{\Trajvar  \vert \Gvar}(\traj\vert \gobs')$, with conditional probabilities given by Bayes' rule, $p_{\Trajvar  \vert \Gvar}(\traj\vert \gobs') = p(\traj) \delta_{\gobs',\gobs(\traj)}/p_{\Gvar}(\gobs')$. 
%{\color{magenta} $q^*_{\Trajvar  \vert \Gvar}(\traj\vert \gobs'):= q^*(\traj) \delta_{\gobs',\gobs(\traj)}/q^*_{\Gvar}(\gobs')=\tilde p_{\Trajvar  \vert \Gvar}(\traj\vert\gobs'):=p(\traj) \delta_{\gobs',\gobs(\traj)}/p_{\Gvar}(\gobs')$.} % where the conditional probability is introduced by Bayes' rule, i.e., $p_{\Trajvar  \vert \Gvar}(\traj\vert \gobs') = p(\traj) \delta_{\gobs',\gobs(\traj)}/p_{\Gvar}(\gobs')$}. 
Using the chain rule of KL divergence, we may further decompose $\epNotG$ into 
\begin{align}
    \epNotG = D(p_{\Gvar}\Vert q^*_{\Gvar})+D(p_{\Trajvar  \vert \Gvar}\Vert \tilde {p}_{\Trajvar  \vert \Gvar})\,.
    \label{eq:decomp2}
\end{align} %while the Pythagorean theorem can be used to show that $D[p(\gobs)\Vert q^*(\gobs)]=D[p(\gobs)\Vert \tilde p (\gobs)]-\epG$. $D[p(\gobs)\Vert \tilde p (\gobs)]$ vanishes when the overall distribution of $\gobs$ is the same under the forward and reverse processes, while   $\epG$ vanishes when the mean of $\gobs$ is the same (see Eq.~\eqref{eq:me0}). 
The first term in Eq.~\eqref{eq:decomp2} vanishes when  $q^*_{\Gvar}=p_{\Gvar}$; it is the additional EP that could be inferred by constraining  all higher-order statistics (not just the mean) in Eq.~\eqref{eq:me0}. The second term is the EP that cannot be inferred from any statistics of $\gobs$.


Finally, our method supports a hierarchical decomposition of EP, where contributions from interactions of increasing order (e.g., pairwise, triplets) are successively added, resulting in a structure $0\le \ep_1 \leq \ep_2 \leq \dots \leq\ep$, with $\Sigma_k=D(q^*_k\Vert \tilde p)$ capturing contributions from functions $\gobs(\traj)$ containing interactions up to order $k$. This structure mirrors information-geometric decompositions in equilibrium MaxEnt \cite{amari2001information,ay2015information} and allows EP to be broken down into interpretable components, each satisfying $\Sigma_k-\Sigma_{k-1}=D(q^*_k\Vert q^*_{k-1})$.

