\PRLsection{Thermodynamic uncertainty relations (TURs)} 
Eq.~\eqref{eq:dual} can be interpreted as a TUR that relates EP and fluctuations of trajectory observables~\cite{horowitz2020thermodynamic}. Consider any trajectory observable that can be expressed as a linear combination $o(\traj)=\params^\top \bm {g}(\traj)$ for some $\params \in \mathbb{R}^d$. The first term in \eqref{eq:dual} is the expectation of $o(\traj)$, while the second term is the cumulant generating function (CGF) of $o(\traj)$ under $\tilde p$. Thus, $\epG$ bounds the ``fluctuation discounted'' expectation of all such $o(\traj)$, giving a higher-order TUR~\cite{dechant2020fluctuation} that constrains all cumulants of trajectory observables. This contrasts with quadratic TURs, which constrain only the mean and  variance~\cite{li2019quantifying,manikandan2020inferring,dechant2018multidimensional}. 

To relate our approach to quadratic TURs, we expand the CGF as $\ln\langle e^{ \params^\top \gobs}\rangle _{\tilde{p}}  \approx    \params^\top \ang{\gobs}_{\tilde p}  +\params^\top \K \params/2$, where $\K$ is the covariance matrix of $\gobs$ under $\tilde p$. Plugging into~\eqref{eq:dual} gives the approximate optimizer $\hat{\params}$ as the solution to the linear system $\K \hat{\params}=\ang{\gobs}_{p} -\ang{\gobs}_{\tilde p}\equiv\ang{\gobs}_{p-\tilde p} $. 
This gives the weaker %(but optimization-free) 
bound \begin{align}
    \epG \ge \GepG &:=    \ang{\gobs}_{p - \tilde p}^\top \Kinv \ang{\gobs}_{p} - \ln  \ang{e^{\ang{\gobs}_{p-\tilde p}^\top \Kinv  \gobs }  }_{\tilde p}.\label{eq:Gaussian-TUR}
\end{align}
% It also gives the trajectory EP approximation, $\hat{\sigma}_{\gobs}(\traj) :=\hat{\params}^{\top}\gobs(\traj)-\ln \langle e^{{\hat{\params}}^\top \gobs}\rangle_{\tilde{p}}$ which satisfies $\langle \hat{\sigma}_{\gobs} \rangle_{p} =\GepG$. 
$\GepG$ can be shown to correspond to a single Newton-Raphson step for maximizing  \eqref{eq:dual} starting from $\params=\bm 0$~\cite{SupplementalMaterial}; it has the advantage of not requiring optimization, only the solution a linear system. 
The bound $\epG\ge \GepG$ becomes tight when $\gobs$ has Gaussian statistics.

%The optimization-free TUR in \eqref{eq:Gaussian-TUR} 
% $ \GepG$ may be compared to
We may also compare our results to a quadratic TUR. 
For stationary systems without odd variables, we find the inequality (see \emph{End Matter})
\begin{align}
%\ep \ge \epGtur \equiv  \frac{1}{2} \langle \gobs \rangle_{p- \tilde p}^{\top}\mathsf{K}_{(p+\tilde{p})/2}^{-1}\langle \gobs \rangle_{p-\tilde p},
\ep \ge \epGtur :=  \frac{1}{2} (\langle \gobs \rangle_{p}-\langle \gobs\rangle_{\tilde p})^{\top}\mathsf{K}_{(p+\tilde{p})/2}^{-1} (\langle \gobs \rangle_{p}-\langle \gobs\rangle_{\tilde p})
\label{eq:GturDef}
\end{align}
where $\mathsf{K}_{(p+\tilde{p})/2}$ is the covariance matrix of $\gobs$ under the mixture distribution $(p+\tilde{p})/2$. 
For antisymmetric observables, Eq.~\eqref{eq:GturDef} may be considered the discrete-time version of the multidimensional TUR from Ref.~\cite{dechant2018multidimensional}.


The bounds~\eqref{eq:Gaussian-TUR}-\eqref{eq:GturDef} may require solving linear systems involving very large covariance matrices. For multipartite systems, it is often possible to split these bounds into $N$ bounds that involve smaller matrices (see \emph{End Matter}).


\PRLsection{Related work}
%Information-theoretic TURs of 
Eq.~\eqref{eq:dual} is closely related to the Donsker-Varadhan  representation from large deviations theory~\cite{donsker1975asymptotic}, previously used to estimate information-theoretic quantities in machine learning~\cite{belghazi2018mine} and thermodynamic inference~\cite{kim2020learning,otsubo2022estimating,grandpre2021entropy}.
% Variational principles of 
%the general form~\eqref{eq:dual} were first proposed in \cite{kim2020learning} and were further explored in~\cite{otsubo2022estimating, kolchinsky2022information, kolchinsky2024generalized}
However, these works did not consider the problem of thermodynamic inference in high-dimensional systems using multivariate observables.
Other approaches to EP inference have employed  compression algorithms~\cite{roldan2010estimating,roldan2012entropy,ro2022model} and deep learning of probability flows~\cite{boffi2024deep}.


Our Pythagorean theorem~\eqref{eq:ep-decomposition} is related to previous decompositions of EP in interacting systems~\cite{ito2020unified} and systems with nonconservative forces~\cite{ito2024geometric,kolchinsky2022information,kolchinsky2024generalized}. 
Moreover, the general form of Eq.~\eqref{eq:me0} is a Maximum Entropy (MaxEnt) problem over trajectory  distributions~\cite{tang2008maximum,marre2009prediction,nguyen2017inverse,cofre2018information}, 
sometimes called ``maximum caliber''~(MaxCal)~\cite{ghosh2020maximum}. 
%\newstuff{Nonequilibrium MaxEnt has been previously used to infer models from which the entropy flow (or the EP under steady-state assumptions) can be estimated---though without ensuring a lower bound \cite{ishihara2025state,aguilera2021unifying}.}
Related techniques have been used to infer models from which the entropy flow can be estimated, though without ensuring a lower bound \cite{ishihara2025state}.

Our approach differs in several other ways from earlier work on MaxEnt and MaxCal. First, we minimize the KL divergence relative to an unknown prior distribution $\tilde p$, from which we typically only have some samples. 
This makes standard approximation methods for large-scale MaxEnt problems (such as mean-field and Bethe approximations~\cite{welling2003approximate,ricci2012bethe,aguilera2023nonequilibrium}) not directly applicable. 
Second, we care not only about the parameters $\params$, as is typical of MaxEnt ``inverse problems'', but also about bounding EP by the quantity $\epG$. 
Both of these issues are resolved by the dual formulation~\eqref{eq:dual}. 





Lynn et al.~\cite{lynn2022decomposing,lynn2022emergence,geli2025non} proposed a way to decompose and bound EP using a different information-theoretic optimization. Although originally focused on local EP in multipartite stationarity systems, it can be generalized to arbitrary observables $\gobs$ and  non-multipartite systems as 
\begin{align}
\epGL & =\min_{q,\tilde q}D(q\Vert\tilde{q})\;\:\text{subject to}\;\: \langle\gobs\rangle_{q}=\langle\gobs\rangle_{p}.\label{eq:lynn}
\end{align}
Here we use assumption of stationarity and no odd variables, %it is restricted to % restricted to  stationary system without odd variables, 
so that  $\tilde{q}(\traj)=q(\revtraj)$. 
Like our bound $\epG$, $\epGL$ can be used to generate hierarchical decompositions of EP. 

Although Eq.~\eqref{eq:lynn} resembles Eq.~\eqref{eq:me0}, it differs in that it simultaneously optimizes both arguments of the KL divergence, $q$ and $\tilde{q}$. 
As we discuss in the \emph{End Matter}, the optimal distribution $q^*$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:lynn} is not in an exponential family and in general it lacks full support. For this reason, Eq.~\eqref{eq:lynn} does not have a tractable dual expression (i.e., analogous to our dual~\eqref{eq:dual}), and 
there is no straightforward way to scale $\epGL$ to large systems. %, because it . %  the way since its Lagrangian requires in principle an exponential number of nonnegative multipliers 
%(see \emph{End Matter}).
