The nonequilibrium spin model is specified by transition probabilities 
\begin{align*}
    T(\bm x_{1}\vert \bm x_{0})=\frac{1}{N} \sum_i\Big[ W_i(\bm x_0) \delta_{\bm x_0,  \bm x_{1}^{[i]}} +(1- W_i(\bm x_0) )\delta_{\bm x_0 ,\bm x_{1}} \Big]
\end{align*}
Here we introduce the spin-flip operator, $(\bm x^{[i]})_i =-x_i$ and $(\bm x^{[i]})_j =x_j$ for $j \neq i$. The flip probability for spin $i$ is
\begin{align}
W_i(\bm x) &= \dfrac{\exp(-\beta x_{i} \sum_{j:j\neq i}w_{ij} x_{j} )}{2\cosh(\beta \sum_{j:j\neq i} w_{ij} x_{j})}\,,
   \label{eq:spin-flip}
\end{align}
where $\beta$ is an inverse temperature and $w_{ij}$ are (typically asymmetric) coupling parameters. 
We consider the diluted version of the model \cite{coolen2001statistical,aurell2011message,zhang2012inference} with $k$ average neighbors. Here  $w_{ij}=c_{ij}z_{ij}/\sqrt{k}$, with  
binary connections $c_{ij}\sim \textrm{Bernoulli}(k/(N-1))$ and real-valued weights $z_{ij}\sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$. 

The steady-state EP in this model %An advantage of this model is that the steady-state EP 
has a simple closed-form expression, % in terms of expectation values: %, providing the ground truth for our EP estimators.  Specifically, 
$\ep=\beta  \sum_{i \ne j}  w_{ij} \langle{(x_{i,1} -x_{i,0}) x_{j,0}}\rangle_{\rm st}$, 
where $\langle \cdot \rangle_{\rm st}$ indicates expectations under the stationary process $p$. 
This provides ground truth to evaluate our estimators. 


