\PRLsection{Multipartite systems}
For multipartite systems, the optimization problem~\eqref{eq:dual} can often be decomposed into a set of smaller problems.  
For simplicity, we consider a single timestep ($T=1$) of a discrete system. 
We also assume multipartite dynamics and multipartite observables. For dynamics, this means that only a single variable $i\in \{1,\dots,N\}$ changes at any one time, 
$p(\traj)=\tilde p(\traj)=0$ whenever both $x_{i,0} \neq  x_{i,1}$ and  $x_{j,0} \ne x_{j,1}$ are satisfied for $i\ne j$.
For observables, the multipartite assumption means that $\gobs$  can be partitioned as 
\begin{align}\gobs=(\gobs_0, \gobs_{1}, \dots, \gobs_{N},\bm a),
\label{eq:gmppartition}
\end{align}
Here  $\gobs_{i}$ for $i\in \{1,\dots,N\}$ specifies  observables that reflect changes of variable $i$ ($\gobs_{i}(\bm x_0, \bm x_1) =\bm 0$ if $x_{i,0}=x_{i,1}$), while  $\gobs_0$ specifies observables that reflect the state not changing ($\gobs_{0}(\bm x_0, \bm x_1) =\bm 0$ if  $\bm x_0\ne \bm x_1$). 
The observables $\bm a =(a_0, a_1,\dots,a_N)$ are  indicator functions for changes of variable $i$,  $a_i(\bm x_0, \bm x_1)=1-\delta_{x_{i,0},x_{i,1}}$. The presence of these indicator observables is an assumption, though not a very strong one as such observables can usually be easily added if not already present in $\gobs$. 
We also define $a_0(\bm x_0, \bm x_1):=1-\sum_{i=1}^N a_i(\bm x_0, \bm x_1)$ as the indicator function for the state not changing.  


Let   ${P}_i := \langle a_i \rangle_{p}$ indicate the probability that variable $i$ changes under the forward process, and $\condpi(\traj):=a_i(\traj) p(\traj)/{P}_i$ the forward distribution conditioned on change of $i$. 
Similarly, ${P}_0 := \langle a_0 \rangle_{p}$ and  
$\condpiZ(\traj):=a_0(\traj) p(\traj)/{P}_0$ 
indicate the probability and conditional distribution of trajectories where the state does not change. 
 $\tilde P_i$ and $\condpirev$ indicate the same quantities under the reverse process $\tilde p$,  and $ Q_i$ and $\condqi$ under a generic trajectory distribution $q$. 
Typically, the probabilities $P_i$ and $\tilde{P}_i$  % in Eq.~\eqref{eq:appfactor0} 
can be easily estimated  from empirical frequencies.


We recall our variational principle~\eqref{eq:me0},
\begin{align}
    \epG &= \min_q D(q\Vert \tilde p) \quad\text{subject to}\quad \langle \gobs \rangle_q = \langle \gobs\rangle_{ p}\,.
    \label{eq:appmpme0}
\end{align}
Without loss of generality, we may restrict the optimization to distributions $q$ with the same multipartite structure, because any $q$ that assigns a strictly positive probability to a non-multipartite transition (more than one variable changes state) has support outside of $\tilde p$ and therefore $D(q\Vert \tilde p)=\infty$. 
Given multipartite $q$ and $\tilde p$, we condition the trajectory distributions on which variable $i$, if any, undergoes a change.  The chain rule for KL divergence then gives
\begin{align}
    D(q\Vert \tilde p) = D(Q\Vert \tilde P)+  
    \sum_{i=0}^N Q_i D(\condqi \Vert \condpirev) \,,
    \label{chainruleKL}
\end{align}
where 
$ D(Q\Vert \tilde P)=\sum_{i=0}^N Q_i \ln (Q_i/\tilde{P}_i)$. 

Given our assumption of multipartite dynamics and observables, we have 
 $\langle \gobs_{i} \rangle_q = Q_i \langle \gobs_{i} \rangle_{\condqi}$ and $\langle \gobs_{i} \rangle_{ p} ={P}_i \langle \gobs_{i} \rangle_{\condpi}$. 
Also, the constraint $\langle \bm a \rangle_q =\langle \bm a \rangle_p$ implied by Eq.~\eqref{eq:appmpme0} means that $Q_i = P_i$. 
Combining allows us to decompose $\epG$ as 
\begin{align}
\epG &=D(P\Vert \tilde P) + \sum_{i=0}^N P_i \epGi\,,
\label{eq:appfactor0}
\end{align}
where we introduced the subproblem  $i\in \{0,\dots,N\}$ as
\begin{align}
    \epGi:=\min_{\condqi} D(\condqi \Vert \condpirev)   \quad\text{subject to}\quad \langle \gobs_{i} \rangle_{\condqi} = \langle \gobs_{i}\rangle_{ \condpi}.
    \label{eq:subproblemapp}
\end{align}
Note that $\langle \bm a \rangle_{\condqi}=\langle \bm a \rangle_{\condpi}$ and $\langle \gobs_j \rangle_{\condqi}=\langle \gobs_j \rangle_{\condpi}$ for $j \neq i$ are automatically satisfied in the subproblem, and so can be dropped from the optimization. 
Each subproblem yields an exponential-form solution and a dual representation, 
\begin{align}
     \epGi = \max_{\params_{i}} \pr{ {\params_{i}}^\top \rang{\gobs_{i}}_{\condpi} - \ln\langle e^{ {\params_{i}}^\top \gobs_{i}}\rangle_{\condpirev}}.
     \label{eq:subprobi}
\end{align}
where ${\params_{i}}$ are the parameters conjugate to $\gobs_{i}$. 

Although our decomposition increases the number of optimization problems, each optimization problem is (typically $\sim N$ times) smaller than Eq.~\eqref{eq:dual}, both in terms of the number of optimization variables and the number of data points used to estimate conditional expectations under $\condpi$ and $\condpirev$.  
Furthermore, we may bound each $\epGi $ using the optimization-free bound $\GepG^{(i)}$ using a smaller covariance matrix than in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Gaussian-TUR}. 



Next, we consider the trajectory EP for multipartite systems. %Given the assumptions above,  
For  any trajectory $\traj$, we may write the trajectory EP as 
\begin{align}
    \sigma(\traj):= \ln\frac{p(\traj)}{\tilde p(\traj)}= \sum_{i=0}^N a_i(\traj) \left[\ln \frac{P_i}{\tilde{P}_i} + \ln\frac{\condpi(\traj)}{\condpirev(\traj)}\right]\,.
    \label{eq:emtrajep}
\end{align}
Observe that $\epG \to \ep$ only if $\epGi \to \ep^{(i)}$ for each $i$. Then, using a maximum likelihood expression similar to  Eq.~\eqref{eq:ml}, we may show that  $\epGi = \ep^{(i)}$ implies 
\begin{align}
\ln\frac{\condpi(\traj)}{\condpirev(\traj)}= \params_i^{*\top} \gobs_{i}(\traj).\label{eq:emlogpi}
\end{align}
Combining Eqs.~\eqref{eq:emtrajep}-\eqref{eq:emlogpi} and  $a_i(\traj) \gobs_i(\traj)= \gobs_i(\traj)$ gives
\begin{align}
    \sigma(\traj)&= \sum_{i=0}^N \left[a_i(\traj)\ln \frac{P_i}{\tilde{P}_i}+\params_i^{*\top} \gobs_{i}(\traj)\right].\label{eq:appfactor2}
\end{align}


For a steady-state system without odd variables,  
$P_i=\langle  a_i \rangle_p =\langle a_i \rangle_{\tilde p}=\tilde{P}_i$ since $p(\traj)=\tilde p (\revtraj)$ and $a_i(\traj)$ is symmetric under time reversal. We also have $\condpiZ = \condpirevZ$, which allows us to ignore the no-change terms corresponding to $i=0$. In this case, our results simplify to
\begin{align}
\epG &= \sum_{i=1}^N P_{i} \epGi \label{eq:appfactor}\\
\sigma(\traj)&= \sum_{i=1}^N \params_i^{*\top} \gobs_{i}(\traj) \quad\text{when} \quad\quad\epG=\ep.\label{eq:appfactortraj}
\end{align}
%Finally, for these systems, we may derive a decomposed version of the multidimensional TUR~\eqref{eq:GturDef} by bounding each term in $\ep = \sum_{i=0}^N P_i \ep^{(i)}$, where $\ep^{(i)} = D(\condpi \Vert \condpirev)$ is the EP due to changes of variable $i$ or no change ($i=0$).  
Finally, for these systems, we may derive a decomposed version of the multidimensional TUR~\eqref{eq:GturDef} by bounding each term in $\ep = \sum_{i=1}^N P_i \ep^{(i)}$, where $\ep^{(i)} = D(\condpi \Vert \condpirev)$ is the EP due to changes of variable $i$.  
