\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:concl}
This work presents upper limits on the isotropic astrophysical neutrino flux at energies above $10^{3.5}$~TeV from searches of neutrino-induced cascades in the 2018--2023 data of Baikal-GVD in the construction stage, equivalent to $\sim 2$ years of the present configuration of the detector. In the lower part of the energy band, these limits are of the same order as those reported by IceCube, and supersede those published by Auger. We further concentrate on the energy range in which the KM3-230213A event was detected. We combine the exposure of Baikal-GVD with those of IceCube, Auger, and KM3NeT, and present the best-fit isotropic $E^{-2}$ flux estimate from the joint analysis. 
With the combined exposure, we also obtain constraints on selected models of cosmogenic neutrinos; other models for the isotropic  neutrino flux can be tested in the same way with the help of the presented energy-dependent effective areas. Future analyses are needed to understand the origin of KM3-230213A and to obtain more information on UHECR sources with the neutrino data.

