Nevertheless, our algorithm is more practical as it uses dynamically updated parameters that do not rely on knowledge of the Lipschitz constant or the noise bound---quantities that are often unavailable or hard to estimate in practice.

% {\color{blue}(ii) When the tail exponent $\alpha$ is not available, Algorithm \ref{alg:unf-sfom-pm} with $\{(\eta_k,\theta_k)\}$ given by \eqref{pm-eta-theta-o} resembles the parameter-free SFOM with momentum proposed in \cite[Appendix D]{hubler2024gradient}. Yet, our complexity analysis, based on establishing descent properties of the potential sequence given by \eqref{def:pot-pm}, is original and significantly different from \cite{hubler2024gradient} and other existing works.
