%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Algebraic stacks have repeatedly proven to be very useful for studying moduli problems. However, like many objects in algebraic geometry, their derived categories remain largely unexplored. As a result, much remains to be understood about the structure and information encoded in the derived category of an algebraic stack.

To set the stage, we focus on the derived category of quasi-coherent sheaves on a quasi-compact quasi-separated algebraic stack $\mathcal{X}$, denoted by $D_{\operatorname{qc}}(\mathcal{X})$. See \Cref{sec:prelim_stacks} for background. Among the possible strategies to study such categories, we focus on $t$-structures and the triangulated subcategory $\operatorname{Perf}(\mathcal{X})$ of perfect complexes.

Over time, it has become clear that perfect complexes and $t$-structures play vital roles in the study of $D_{\operatorname{qc}}$ in the setting of schemes. For example, the regularity of a Noetherian scheme is controlled by the $t$-structures on $\operatorname{Perf}$ \cite{Neeman:2024,Smith:2022,Clark/Lank/ManaliRahul/Parker:2024}. Similar observations have recently been made for algebraic stacks as well \cite{DeDeyn/Lank/ManaliRahul/Peng:2025}. 

The study of $t$-structures on schemes has been a very interesting topic, and we have learned several important results along the way. To begin with, a complete classification of $t$-structures on affine schemes is known to be impossible \cite{Stanley:2010}. This naturally leads to imposing additional conditions on $t$-structures in hopes of obtaining a classification.

One such approach is to focus on $t$-structures that are compactly generated. Initially, this restriction allowed for a classification in the affine Noetherian setting \cite{AlsonoTarrio/JeremiasLopez/Saorin:2010}. These results have also been extended to Noetherian schemes under an additional `tensor compatibility' condition \cite{Dubey/Sahoo:2023}. Interestingly, the Noetherian assumption was dropped for the affine case \cite{Hrbek:2020}, which is a key ingredient to this work. In fact, a very nice novelty of our work is that using only the affine results of \cite{Hrbek:2020}, we can go directly to algebraic stacks without any need of classifications from \cite{Dubey/Sahoo:2023}.

All these classifications involve what are called `Thomason filtrations'. These are certain decreasing functions from the integers to the collection of Thomason subsets of the underlying topological space (see \Cref{sec:prelim_Thomason} for details). However, no analogous classification has yet been established for algebraic stacks.

This brings attention to the main result.

\begin{introthm}
    [see \Cref{thm:stacky_DB}]
    \label{introthm:stacky_DB}
    Let $\mathcal{X}$ be a concentrated Thomason quasi-compact quasi-separated algebraic stack. If $D^{\leq 0}_{\operatorname{qc}}(\mathcal{X})$ is compactly generated, then there is a one-to-one correspondence:
    \begin{displaymath}
        \begin{aligned}
            \{ \otimes\textrm{-aisle on } & D_{\operatorname{qc}}(\mathcal{X}) \textrm{ generated by } \mathcal{P} \subseteq \operatorname{Perf}(\mathcal{X}) \} 
            \\&\iff \{ \textrm{Thomason filtrations on } \mathcal{X} \textrm{ supported by perfects} \}.
        \end{aligned}
    \end{displaymath}
    In particular, for any $\otimes$-aisle on $D_{\operatorname{qc}}(\mathcal{X})$ generated by $\mathcal{P} \subseteq \operatorname{Perf}(\mathcal{X})$, we assign to it the Thomason filtration given by the rule (which is independent of the choice for $\mathcal{P}$)
    \begin{displaymath}
        n\mapsto \bigcup_{\substack{j\geq n\\ P\in \mathcal{P}}} \operatorname{supp}(\mathcal{H}^j (P)).
    \end{displaymath}
\end{introthm}

The hypothesis of \Cref{introthm:stacky_DB} with $D^{\leq 0}_{\operatorname{qc}}(\mathcal{X})$ being compactly generated is relatively mild in nature. For instance, it is satisfied if $\mathcal{X}$ also enjoys the resolution property (see \Cref{cor:resolution_property_compactly_generated_standard_aisle}). This includes tame Deligne--Mumford stacks with a finite diagonal 
%% So diagonal is quasi-finite, affine, separated and has finite stabilizer groups
and which can be identified as the quotient stack $[U/\operatorname{GL}_{N,\mathbb{Z}}]$ for some affine scheme $U$. See \Cref{ex:stacks_concentrated,ex:stacks_Thomason,ex:stacks_resolution_property,ex:stacks_approximation_by_compacts} for details.

A novel and significant aspect of this work is that our proof of \Cref{thm:stacky_DB} relies solely on Hrbek's classification for affine schemes \cite{Hrbek:2020}. This approach entirely bypasses any dependence on the scheme-theoretic results of \cite{Dubey/Sahoo:2023}. In fact, as a consequence of \Cref{introthm:stacky_DB}, we obtain an independent proof of \cite[Theorem 5.11]{Dubey/Sahoo:2023} when a Noetherian scheme has the resolution property, which will be discussed shortly. 

The condition for a Thomason filtration to be `supported by perfects' is defined in \Cref{def:graded_filtration}. As a first step towards \Cref{introthm:stacky_DB}, we show for an affine scheme that every Thomason filtration is supported by perfects. Then we establish auxiliary result (see \Cref{prop:pullback_t_exactness}). It allows us to better understand such $t$-structures by passing to an affine scheme that is a smooth cover of the algebraic stack. This immediately enables us to bootstrap the classification known in the affine setting thanks to Hrbek \cite{Hrbek:2020}.

Next a particularly pleasant consequence is a sharper classification.

\begin{introcor}
    [see \Cref{cor:stacky_concentrated}]
    \label{introcor:stacky_concentrated}
    Let $\mathcal{X}$ be a concentrated Noetherian algebraic stack satisfying the resolution property. Then there is a one-to-one correspondence:
    \begin{displaymath}
        \begin{aligned}
            \{ \textrm{compactly generated } \otimes\textrm{-aisle on } & D_{\operatorname{qc}}(\mathcal{X})\} 
            \\&\iff \{ \textrm{Thomason filtrations on } \mathcal{X} \}.
        \end{aligned}
    \end{displaymath}
\end{introcor}

There are concrete settings for which \Cref{cor:stacky_concentrated} is applicable. It includes when $X$ also has quasi-finite separated diagonal or is a Deligne--Mumford stack over $\mathbb{Q}$ (see \cite[Theorem A]{Hall/Lamarche/Lank/Peng:2025}). The key ingredient, introduced in \cite{Hall/Lamarche/Lank/Peng:2025}, is a notion called `approximation by compacts'. This condition allows us to leverage \Cref{thm:stacky_DB} to show every Thomason filtration is in fact supported by perfects for such algebraic stack. As stated earlier, this gives an independent proof of \cite[Theorem 5.11]{Dubey/Sahoo:2023} when $X$ is a Noetherian scheme with the resolution property. However, it does so more generally for any Noetherian scheme $X$ such that $D^{\leq 0}_{\operatorname{qc}}(X)$ is compactly generated. 

