\begin{document}

\title{A modern perspective on rational homotopy theory}
\author{Eleftherios Chatzitheodoridis}
\address{Department of Mathematics, University of Virginia, Charlottesville, VA 22904} \email{thp5uc@virginia.edu}
\date{\today}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
In Quillen's paper on rational homotopy theory, the category of $1$-reduced simplicial sets is endowed with a family of model structures, the most prominent of which is the one whose weak equivalences are the rational homotopy equivalences. In this paper, we give a modern approach to this family of model structures.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}\label{section1}
\noindent Rational homotopy theory studies $1$-connected spaces by simplifying the computation of their homotopy groups. The non-vanishing homotopy groups of a $1$-connected space are abelian, so their torsion can be discarded by tensoring with the rational numbers. Discarding torsion makes homotopy group computations more tractable: in the case of spheres, their rational homotopy groups were computed by Serre in \cite{Serre1951}. The success of rational homotopy theory has led to its extension to nilpotent spaces by Bousfield and Kan in \cite{BousfieldKan1972}; Sullivan in \cite{Sullivan1974} and \cite{Sullivan1977}; Hilton, Mislin, and Roitberg in \cite{HMR1975}; Bousfield and Gugenheim in \cite{BousfieldGugenheim1976}; and Neisendorfer in \cite{Neisendorfer1978}. Ultimately, rational homotopy theory has been extended to all spaces by Casacuberta and Peschke in \cite{CasacubertaPeschke1993}; G{\'{o}}mez-Tato, Halperin, and Tanr{\'{e}} in \cite{GTHT2000}; and Bastardas and Casacuberta in \cite{BastardasCasacuberta2001}. \\ \\
Model categories, as introduced by Quillen in \cite{Quillen1967}, provide a formal framework in which many tools and constructions from homotopy theory are defined. For instance, every model category admits a homotopy category, which is the localization of the model category at its class of weak equivalences. Thus, a model structure on a category endows the category with a homotopy theory and gives us tools to study that homotopy theory. \\ \\
In \cite{Quillen1969}, Quillen applies his recently introduced notion of a model category to study rational homotopy theory, and especially how rational homotopy theory translates difficult problems in geometry and topology to more feasible problems in algebra.  More specifically, Quillen produces model categories both on the topological side and on the algebraic side, and then proves that specific functorial constructions between them induce equivalences of these model categories. The notion of equivalence that we are referring to is that of a Quillen equivalence; in particular, a Quillen equivalence induces an equivalence of homotopy categories. On the algebraic side, Quillen produces model structures on rational differential graded (DG) Lie algebras and rational DG coalgebras, which are important algebraic invariants for computations in rational homotopy theory. \\ \\
In this paper, we focus on the topological side, where Quillen produces a family of model structures on the category of $1$-reduced simplicial sets: simplicial sets whose $1$-skeleton is a point. As the category of $1$-connected spaces is ill-behaved with respect to important types of limits and colimits, such as pullbacks, the passage to $1$-reduced simplicial sets is necessary for Quillen's model-categorical approach to rational homotopy theory. \\ \\
This family of model structures interpolates between ordinary homotopy theory, where the weak equivalences are weak homotopy equivalences, and rational homotopy theory, where the weak equivalences are rational homotopy equivalences. Namely, for a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, Quillen endows the category of $1$-reduced simplicial sets with a model structure in which a map is a weak equivalence if and only if it induces an isomorphism of homotopy groups after tensoring with $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$; we call such a map an $M$-local homotopy equivalence. \\ \\
Tensoring with $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$ for different choices of a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$ allows us to preserve specific types of torsion in homotopy groups. For instance, for a prime $p$, taking $M=\mathbb{Z} \setminus p\mathbb{Z}$ and tensoring with the $p$-local integers $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ specifically preserves $p$-torsion. Thus, we can study the torsion one prime at a time. \\ \\
The following table depicts the interpolation given by varying our choice of a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$.

\begin{table}[H]
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
{\color{blue}{$M$}}                                                                       & $\{1\}$      & $\mathbb{Z} \setminus 2\mathbb{Z}$ & $\mathbb{Z} \setminus \{0\}$ \\ \hline
{\color{blue}{$M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$}}                                                        & $\mathbb{Z}$ & $\mathbb{Z}_{(2)}$                 & $\mathbb{Q}$                 \\ \hline
{\color{blue}{torsion discarded by $- \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$}} & none         & all but $2$-torsion                & all                          \\ \hline
{\color{blue}{$M$-local homotopy equivalence}}                                                         & weak         & $2$-local                          & rational                     \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\label{table1}
\end{table}
\noindent To establish this family of model structures, Quillen verifies the model category axioms one-by-one. In later years, though, further advancements in the study of model categories have provided us with powerful tools that enable us to induce new model structures from old without needing to perform such a painstaking verification. \\ \\
The motivating idea behind our work in this paper is that we can use these modern tools to provide a new approach to Quillen's family of model structures on $1$-reduced simplicial sets. The first tool we employ is that of left transfer, as developed by Bayeh, Hess, Karpova, K{\k{e}}dziorek, Riehl, and Shipley in \cite{BHKKRS2015}. This tool allows us to transfer the model structure on pointed simplicial sets to a model structure on $1$-reduced simplicial sets whose weak equivalences are the weak homotopy equivalences. Then, we left Bousfield localize the latter model structure to obtain the rest of Quillen's family. Although the tool of left Bousfield localization dates back to the work of Bousfield in \cite{Bousfield1975} and \cite{Bousfield1977} and Farjoun in \cite{Farjoun1992} and \cite{Farjoun1995}, we rely on the more recent existence results for left Bousfield localization developed by Hirschhorn in \cite{Hirschhorn2003} and by Barwick in \cite{Barwick2010}. \\ \\
For the left transfer, we observe that a $1$-reduced simplicial set has a unique $0$-simplex, so there is a forgetful functor from the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ of $1$-reduced simplicial sets to the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ of pointed simplicial sets. This functor admits a right adjoint given by the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex of a pointed simplicial set as defined by May in \cite{May1967}; in fact, Quillen uses this construction to establish his family of model structures. As the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex depends on the basepoint, the passage to pointed simplicial sets, rather than unpointed ones, is necessary. We use this adjunction to left-transfer the model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ to the following model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\begin{theorem}\label{intro1} There is a model structure on the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is a weak homotopy equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}

\end{theorem}
\noindent Then, for the left Bousfield localization, we characterize, for a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, the $M$-local spaces and the $M$-local homotopy equivalences in the language of function complexes. Our characterizations can be viewed as the enriched-in-simplicial-sets version of the work of Casacuberta, Peschke, and Pfenniger in \cite{CPP1992}, which uses hom-sets rather than function complexes. Using these characterizations, we left Bousfield localize the model category from Theorem \ref{intro1} to recover the entire family of model structures on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ from \cite{Quillen1969}. We say that a simplicial set $X$ is $M$-local if $X$ is a Kan complex whose higher homotopy groups are $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$-modules.

\begin{theorem}\label{intro2} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ on the category of $1$-reduced simplicial sets in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent A $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is fibrant in $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ if and only if $X$ is $M$-local.
\end{theorem}
\noindent We also give an alternative description of this family of model structures using Bousfield localization with respect to homology, as introduced in \cite{Bousfield1975}, in conjunction with the result on left-induced model structures from \cite{BHKKRS2015} that we use for Theorem \ref{intro1}. The idea is that, rather than first left-transfer the model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ and then localize, we can try to localize the model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ with respect to homology with $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$-coefficients and then left-transfer this localized model structure, which we denote by $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}$, to ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. We show that this approach also yields the family of model structures from Theorem \ref{intro2}. Schematically, the diagram of model-categorical operations
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=6mm, column sep=6mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}} \&\& {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{1}} \\
	\\
	{\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}} \&\& {\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{1}}
	\arrow["{\textrm{localization}}"', squiggly, from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{\textrm{transfer}}", squiggly, from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{\textrm{localization}}", squiggly, from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["{\textrm{transfer}}", squiggly, from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent commutes, thus giving us two modern descriptions of the family of model structures on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. \\ \\
Another motivating goal of ours is to show that these model structures have properties that one generally desires and expects in the context of model categories. The tools that we invoke guarantee us most of these properties. An exception is the property of being a simplicial model category, as defined by Quillen in \cite{Quillen1967}. We establish this property explicitly by adapting the techniques of Goerss and Jardine in \cite{GoerssJardine1999} to show that the model category from Theorem \ref{intro1} is simplicial. As left Bousfield localization preserves the simplicial structure, this structure is bestowed upon the entire family of model structures on $1$-reduced simplicial sets from Theorem \ref{intro2}. We remark that, in \cite{Burke2021}, Burke proves the analog of our simplicial structure for reduced simplicial sets: simplicial sets with a unique $0$-simplex. However, Burke's line of argument is different from that in this paper. \\ \\
Also, we partially extend Theorem \ref{intro2} to the category $\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ of simplicial sets. Namely, we produce a family of model structures on simplicial sets that describes a generalization of rational homotopy theory to all spaces. In \cite{Ivanov2022}, Ivanov gives an overview of the various approaches to rational homotopy theory beyond the framework of $1$-connectedness or nilpotency. We adopt the approach of G{\'{o}}mez-Tato, Halperin, and Tanr{\'{e}} from \cite{GTHT2000}. In this approach, one rationalizes a connected space $X$ by applying fiberwise rationalization to the fibration $$\widetilde{X} \rightarrow X \rightarrow K(\pi_{1}(X), 1)$$
given by the $1$st Postnikov approximation of $X$; its homotopy fiber $\widetilde{X}$ is the universal cover of $X$, which is a $1$-connected space that can be rationalized in the classical sense. The tool of fiberwise localization is invoked by Sullivan in \cite{Sullivan1974} and investigated by Llerena in \cite{Llerena1982} and \cite{Llerena1985} and Farjoun in \cite{Farjoun1995_CI}. This rationalization theory for non-nilpotent spaces is also studied in recent work of Rivera, Wierstra, and Zeinalian in \cite{RWZ2021}. \\ \\
We partially extend Theorem \ref{intro2} to all simplicial sets as follows.

\begin{theorem}\label{intro3} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ on the category of simplicial sets in which a simplicial set $X$ is fibrant if and only if $X$ is $M$-local.
\end{theorem}
\noindent We also prove the counterpart of this theorem for the category ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ of topological spaces. The reason why Theorem \ref{intro3} only partially extends Theorem \ref{intro2} to all simplicial sets is that we lack a description of the weak equivalences other than the standard one for a left Bousfield localization. This deficiency is due to the fact that fiberwise rationalization satisfies a more intricate universal property than that of the rationalization of $1$-connected spaces, which causes our characterization of the weak equivalences in Theorem \ref{intro2} to break down. \\ \\
Lastly, we verify that our localization $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ of spaces with respect to rational homotopy from Theorem \ref{intro3} does not coincide with Bousfield's localization of spaces with respect to rational homology from \cite{Bousfield1975}. This result captures the fact that rational homotopy and rational homology do not agree beyond the framework of $1$-connected spaces; in fact, our proof of the result involves a specific instance of this general phenomenon.

\subsection*{Organization of the paper} Section \ref{section2} comprises background on $1$-reduced simplicial sets. In Section \ref{section3}, we perform the left transfer to obtain Theorem \ref{intro1}. In Section \ref{section4}, we prove some results about the fibrations in this left-induced model structure. In Section \ref{section5}, we establish that the model category from Theorem \ref{intro1} is simplicial. Section \ref{section6} consists of auxiliary characterization results from the rational homotopy theory of $1$-connected spaces. We use these results in Section \ref{section7} to perform the left Bousfield localization that yields Theorem \ref{intro2}. In Section \ref{section8}, we give an alternative approach to Theorem \ref{intro2} via Bousfield localization with respect to homology. In Section \ref{section9}, we give an overview of rational homotopy theory beyond the setting of $1$-connectedness. In Section \ref{section10}, we perform the left Bousfield localization that yields Theorem \ref{intro3}. In Section \ref{section11}, we establish the analog of Theorem \ref{intro3} for topological spaces. Lastly, in Section \ref{section12}, we show that our localization of spaces with respect to rational homotopy is not equal to the Bousfield localization of spaces with respect to rational homology.

