\section{$1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section2}
\noindent We begin with some background on $1$-reduced simplicial sets. We define the $1$-skeleton and the $1$-coskeleton of a simplicial set. Using these notions, we define $1$-reduced simplicial sets. Then, we define the {$1$-reduction} of a simplicial set, as well as the {$1$st Eilenberg subcomplex} of a pointed simplicial set. These constructions provide us with adjunctions between $1$-reduced simplicial sets and (pointed) simplicial sets that we rely on in this paper.

\subsection{Simplicial sets}\label{section2.1} We collect background and notation on simplicial sets; a reference is \cite[Chapter I]{GoerssJardine1999}. \\ \\
The combinatorics involved in the definition of a simplicial set can be packaged in the simplex category $\Delta$.

\begin{definition}\label{defsimpcatdef} The \emph{simplex category} $\Delta$ is the category whose

\begin{enumerate}

\item objects are the sets $[n]=\{0, 1, \dots, n\}$ for a non-negative integer $n$; and

\item morphisms are the order-preserving maps $\theta \colon [n] \rightarrow [m]$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{definition}

\begin{example}\label{defsimpcatdef1} For $0 \leq i \leq n$, two special types of maps in $\Delta$ are

\begin{enumerate}

\item the $i$-th \emph{coface} map $d^{i} \colon [n-1] \rightarrow [n]$; and

\item the $i$-th \emph{codegeneracy} map $s^{i} \colon [n+1] \rightarrow [n]$.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent These maps generate the simplex category $\Delta$ subject to a list of identities that determine their composites.
\end{example}
\noindent The simplex category $\Delta$ allows for a concise definition of a simplicial set. We write ${\mathcal{S}\mathrm{ets}}$ for the category of sets.

\begin{definition}\label{defssetdef} A \emph{simplicial set} is a functor $X \colon \Delta^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{S}\mathrm{ets}}$. In detail, a simplicial set comprises

\begin{enumerate}

\item a set of \emph{$n$-simplices} $X_{n}$ for every non-negative integer $n$;

\item \emph{face} maps $d_{i} \colon X_{n} \rightarrow X_{n-1}$ for $0 \leq i \leq n$; and

\item \emph{degeneracy} maps $s_{i} \colon X_{n} \rightarrow X_{n+1}$ for $0 \leq i \leq n$;
\end{enumerate}
\noindent subject to a list of simplicial identities that govern how the faces and degeneracies are composed.

\end{definition}
\noindent We write ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ for the category of simplicial sets and simplicial maps (natural transformations) between them.

\begin{example}\label{defssetdef1} The \emph{standard $n$-simplex} is the representable simplicial set $$\Delta[n]=\operatorname{Hom}_{\Delta}\left(-, [n]\right).$$ The identity $\operatorname{id}_{[n]}$ gives an $n$-simplex $\sigma_{n}$ of $\Delta[n]$. For a simplicial set $X$, the Yoneda lemma yields the bijection $$X_{n} \cong \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}}\left(\Delta[n], X\right)$$
that is natural in $[n]$ and in $X$. Therefore, we occasionally view an $n$-simplex of $X$ as a map $\Delta[n] \rightarrow X$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}\label{defssetdef2} The \emph{boundary} $\partial\Delta[n]$ is the smallest subcomplex of $\Delta[n]$ containing the faces $d_{i}(\sigma_{n})$ for $0 \leq i \leq n$. We write $$i_{n} \colon \partial\Delta[n] \rightarrow \Delta[n]$$ for the boundary inclusion.
\end{example}

\begin{example}\label{defssetdef3} For $n \geq 1$ and $0 \leq k \leq n$, the \emph{horn} $\Lambda[n, k]$ is the smallest subcomplex of $\Delta[n]$ containing $d_{i}(\sigma_{n})$ for $i \neq k$. We write $$j_{n, k} \colon \Lambda[n, k] \rightarrow \Delta[n]$$ for the horn inclusion. Observe that horns are undefined for $n=0$.
\end{example}
\noindent We can geometrically realize a simplicial set $X$ to obtain a CW complex $|X|$. Writing ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ for the category of topological spaces, we denote the geometric realization functor by $$|-| \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}.$$
Conversely, for a topological space $Y$, its {singular complex} $\operatorname{Sing}(Y)$ is the simplicial set with $n$-simplices $$\operatorname{Sing}(Y)_{n}=\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(|\Delta[n]|, Y\right).$$
This pair of functors forms an adjunction that we use to oscillate between simplicial sets and topological spaces.
\begin{proposition}[{\cite[Chapter I, Proposition 2.2]{GoerssJardine1999}}]\label{actuallysimpadj} The geometric realization functor $|-| \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ is left adjoint to the singular complex functor $\operatorname{Sing} \colon {\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$.
\end{proposition}
\noindent Our work largely involves pointed simplicial sets, which are simplicial sets with a designated $0$-simplex as their basepoint. We write ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ for the category of pointed simplicial sets and pointed simplicial maps between them. The following adjunction is verified just like its analog in topological spaces.

\begin{lemma}\label{pointtheunpointed} The forgetful functor $V \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ has a left adjoint $$(-)_{+} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$$ defined by attaching a disjoint basepoint.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{The $1$-skeleton and the $1$-coskeleton of a simplicial set}\label{section2.2} To define $1$-reduced simplicial sets, we recall the $1$-skeleton of a simplicial set. We also recall the $1$-coskeleton of a simplicial set, which we refer to in the definition of the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex of a pointed simplicial set. Our reference is \cite[Chapter 2.6]{Bergner2018}. \\ \\
Let $\Delta_{1}$ denote the full subcategory of the simplex category $\Delta$ with objects $[0]$ and $[1]$. The inclusion $$\Delta_{1} \rightarrow \Delta$$ induces a truncation functor $$\operatorname{tr}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{S}\mathrm{ets}}^{\Delta_{1}^{\operatorname{op}}}.$$
This truncation functor admits both a left adjoint
$$s_{1} \colon {\mathcal{S}\mathrm{ets}}^{\Delta_{1}^{\operatorname{op}}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$$ and a right adjoint
$$c_{1} \colon {\mathcal{S}\mathrm{ets}}^{\Delta_{1}^{\operatorname{op}}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}},$$
in terms of which we can define the $1$-skeleton and the $1$-coskeleton of a simplicial set.

\begin{definition}\label{def1skeletondef} Let $X$ be a simplicial set.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The \emph{$1$-skeleton} of $X$ is the simplicial set $$\operatorname{sk}_{1}(X)=s_{1}\operatorname{tr}_{1}(X).$$

\item The \emph{$1$-coskeleton} of $X$ is the simplicial set $$\operatorname{cosk}_{1}(X)=c_{1}\operatorname{tr}_{1}(X).$$

\end{enumerate}
\noindent In detail, both $\operatorname{sk}_{1}(X)$ and $\operatorname{cosk}_{1}(X)$ have set of $0$-simplices $X_{0}$ and set of $1$-simplices $X_{1}$, but they differ in their higher-dimensional simplices. The higher-dimensional simplices of $\operatorname{sk}_{1}(X)$ are the iterated degeneracies of its $1$-simplices. On the contrary, $\operatorname{cosk}_{1}(X)$ has an $n$-simplex for every compatible collection of $(n-1)$-simplices. Equivalently, $\operatorname{cosk}_{1}(X)$ has an $n$-simplex for every map $\operatorname{sk}_{1}(\Delta[n]) \rightarrow X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{def1skeletondef0} It follows from this definition that, for a simplicial set $X$, we have an inclusion map $$\operatorname{sk}_{1}(X) \rightarrow X$$ and a quotient map $$X \rightarrow \operatorname{cosk}_{1}(X),$$ where the latter map identifies the simplices of $X$ that share the same $1$-skeleton.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}\label{def1skeletondef1} Observe that the $1$-skeleton of the standard $2$-simplex $\Delta[2]$ is its boundary $\partial \Delta[2]$.
\end{example}

\subsection{$1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section2.3} We are now ready to define $1$-reduced simplicial sets.

\begin{definition}\label{defreduceddef} A simplicial set $X$ is \emph{$1$-reduced} if its $1$-skeleton $\operatorname{sk}_{1}(X)$ is a point. In detail, a simplicial set $X$ is $1$-reduced if it has a unique $0$-simplex whose degeneracy is the unique $1$-simplex of $X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{defreducedrefrmk} Note that the geometric realization of a $1$-reduced simplicial set is a $1$-connected CW complex. It is also crucial to observe that a $1$-reduced simplicial set is unambiguously pointed, for it has a unique $0$-simplex.
\end{remark}
\noindent We write ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ for the category of $1$-reduced simplicial sets and simplicial maps between them.

\begin{example}\label{sothisdoeswork} For $n \geq 2$, the \emph{simplicial $n$-sphere} $$S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}=\Delta[n]/\partial\Delta[n]$$ is a $1$-reduced simplicial set consisting of a unique $0$-simplex and a unique non-degenerate $n$-simplex.
\end{example}
\noindent The proof of the following lemma is straightforward. We write $\wedge$ for the smash product.

\begin{lemma}\label{smashingtensor} If $X$ is a $1$-reduced simplicial set and $Z$ is a pointed simplicial set, then $X \wedge Z$ is $1$-reduced.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{The $1$-reduction of a simplicial set}\label{section2.4}
\noindent We relate the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ of $1$-reduced simplicial sets to the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ of simplicial sets by means of a left adjoint to the forgetful functor $U \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$. This left adjoint quotients out the $1$-skeleton of a simplicial set to yield a $1$-reduced simplicial set. Although quotients can be homotopically ill-behaved, this adjunction aids our model-categorical arguments later in this paper, for it repeatedly lets us translate lifting problems that we wish to solve into lifting problems that we can solve.

\begin{definition}\label{defreductiondef} The \emph{$1$-reduction} of a simplicial set $X$ is the $1$-reduced simplicial set $$R_{1}(X)=X/\operatorname{sk}_{1}(X).$$
\end{definition}

\begin{example}\label{defreductiondef2} The simplicial $2$-sphere is $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}=R_{1}(\Delta[2])$ because, by Example \ref{def1skeletondef1}, $\operatorname{sk}_{1}(\Delta[2])=\partial\Delta[2]$.
\end{example}
\noindent The $1$-reduction of a simplicial set is its largest $1$-reduced quotient in the sense of the following universal property, which is an instance of the universal property of quotients.

\begin{lemma}\label{unproprreduction} For every map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ from a simplicial set $X$ to a $1$-reduced simplicial set $Y$, there exists a unique map $\overline{f} \colon R_{1}(X) \rightarrow Y$ such that the diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	X \&\& {R_{1}(X)} \\
	\\
	\&\& {Y}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["{\overline{f}}"', dashed, from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent commutes, where $X \rightarrow R_{1}(X)$ is the quotient map from $X$ to $R_{1}(X)=X/\operatorname{sk}_{1}(X)$.
\end{lemma}
\noindent This universal property translates to the promised adjunction.

\begin{lemma}\label{notquite} The $1$-reduction ${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is left adjoint to the forgetful functor $U \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$.
\end{lemma}
\noindent We note a consequence of this adjunction for later use.

\begin{corollary}\label{notquitesimplices} If $X$ is a $1$-reduced simplicial set, then a map $\Delta[2] \rightarrow X$ is adjoint to a map $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2} \rightarrow X$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof} The claim is a consequence of Lemma \ref{notquite} because $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}=R_{1}(\Delta[2])$ by Example \ref{defreductiondef2}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex of a pointed simplicial set}\label{section2.5}
\noindent As we shall see, the approach of taking the $1$-reduction of a simplicial set does not play as nicely as we need it to for our homotopy-theoretic purposes, but we can do better. Because a $1$-reduced simplicial set has a unique $0$-simplex, there is a forgetful functor $$F \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$$ from the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ of $1$-reduced simplicial sets to the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ of pointed simplicial sets. This functor has not only a left adjoint given by $1$-reduction, but also a right adjoint given by the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex of a pointed simplicial set. We present its definition, though we do not use it beyond this section, as its only feature that we need in this paper is the aforementioned adjunction, which we establish. \\ \\
Recall from Remark \ref{def1skeletondef0} that, for a simplicial set $X$, we have a quotient map $X \rightarrow \operatorname{cosk}_{1}(X)$.

\begin{definition}[{\cite[Definition 8.3]{May1967}}]\label{defeilenbergsubdef} The \emph{$1$st Eilenberg subcomplex} $E_{1}(X)$ of a pointed simplicial set $X$ with basepoint $x_{0}$ is defined by the pullback square

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{E_{1}(X)} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{\Delta[0]} \&\& {\operatorname{cosk}_{1}(X).}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["{x_{0}}", from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent In detail, $E_{1}(X)$ consists of all simplices $\chi$ of $X$ such that every $0$-dimensional face of $\chi$ is $x_{0}$ and every $1$-dimensional face of $\chi$ is the degeneracy of $x_{0}$.
\end{definition}
\noindent It follows from this definition that the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex $E_{1}(X)$ of a pointed simplicial set $X$ is $1$-reduced. \\ \\
The $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex of a pointed simplicial set is its largest $1$-reduced subcomplex in the sense of the following universal property that is straightforward to verify.

\begin{lemma}\label{unproprEilenberg} Every pointed map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ from a $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ to a pointed simplicial set $Y$ maps into the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex $E_{1}(Y)$ of $Y$. Thus, there is a unique map $\hat{f} \colon X \rightarrow E_{1}(Y)$ such that the diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	X \&\& {E_{1}(Y)} \\
	\\
	\&\& {Y}
	\arrow["{\hat{f}}", dashed, from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent commutes, where $E_{1}(Y) \rightarrow Y$ is the inclusion map.
\end{lemma}
\noindent This universal property amounts to the promised adjunction, which is stated in Part (2) of the following lemma.
\begin{lemma}\label{yesquite} Let $F \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ denote the forgetful functor.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The $1$-reduction functor ${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is left adjoint to $F$.

\item The $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex functor $E_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is right adjoint to $F$.
\end{enumerate}

\end{lemma}
\noindent Lemma \ref{yesquite} implies that small limits and colimits in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ are constructed as in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$. Given a diagram $$L \colon \mathcal{D} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$$ in $1$-reduced simplicial sets, we form its (co)limit by post-composing $L$ with the forgetful functor $$F \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$$ and forming the (co)limit of the diagram $$FL \colon \mathcal{D} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$$ in pointed simplicial sets. This (co)limit is a $1$-reduced simplicial set, and all the structure maps are in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. \\ \\
In particular, the initial object of ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is the point $\Delta[0]$, not the empty simplicial set $\emptyset$. Thus, the forgetful functor $U \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ does not preserve the initial object, so it cannot admit a right adjoint.

\section{The left transfer to $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section3}
\noindent We transfer the model structure on the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ of pointed simplicial sets along the adjunction $$F \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \colon E_{1}$$ to a model structure on the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ of $1$-reduced simplicial sets whose weak equivalences are the weak homotopy equivalences. This left transfer is an application of \cite[Theorem 2.23]{BHKKRS2015}.

\subsection{Model categories}\label{section3.1} We collect some background on model categories from \cite[\S 3]{DwyerSpalinski1995} and \cite[Chapters 7, 11, and 13]{Hirschhorn2003}. We first recall a concept in the homotopy theory of simplicial sets from \cite[Chapter I]{GoerssJardine1999}.
\begin{definition}\label{defkanfibrdef} A \emph{Kan fibration} is a simplicial map $p \colon X \rightarrow Y$ that has the right lifting property with respect to the set of all horn inclusions
$$J=\left\{j_{n, k} \colon \Lambda[n, k] \rightarrow \Delta[n] \mid n \geq 1 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\}.$$
In other words, a simplicial map $p \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is a Kan fibration if every lifting problem of the form
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Lambda[n, k]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n]} \&\& Y
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{j_{n, k}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["p"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent can be solved. A \emph{Kan complex} is a simplicial set $X$ such that the unique map $X \rightarrow \Delta[0]$ is a Kan fibration.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[{\cite[Chapter I, Lemma 3.3]{GoerssJardine1999}}]\label{defkanfibrdef2} If $Y$ is a space, then $\operatorname{Sing}(Y)$ is a Kan complex.
\end{example}
\noindent The homotopy theory of simplicial sets can be studied systematically in the framework of model categories.

\begin{definition}\label{defmodelcatdef} A \emph{model category} comprises a category $\mathcal{M}$ that has all finite limits and colimits, as well as three distinguished classes of morphisms in $\mathcal{M}$: a class of \emph{weak equivalences}, a class of \emph{cofibrations}, and a class of \emph{fibrations}. A weak equivalence that is also a cofibration is called an \emph{acyclic cofibration}. A weak equivalence that is also a fibration is called an \emph{acyclic fibration}. This data satisfies the axioms in \cite[Definition 3.3]{DwyerSpalinski1995}.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{defmodelcatdefrmk2} In a model category, the weak equivalences and the fibrations together determine the cofibrations. Dually, the weak equivalences and the cofibrations together determine the fibrations.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}\label{defmodelcatdefrmk1} Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a model category with initial object $\emptyset$ and terminal object $\ast$. An object $X$ of $\mathcal{M}$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item \emph{cofibrant} if the unique morphism $\emptyset \rightarrow X$ is a cofibration; and

\item \emph{fibrant} if the unique morphism $X \rightarrow \ast$ is a fibration.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Then, for an object $Y$ of $\mathcal{M}$,

\begin{enumerate}

\item a \emph{cofibrant replacement} of $Y$ is a weak equivalence $A \rightarrow Y$ with cofibrant source $A$; and

\item a \emph{fibrant replacement} of $Y$ is a weak equivalence $Y \rightarrow B$ with fibrant target $B$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{remark}

\begin{example}[{\cite[Theorems 7.10.12 and 7.10.13]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{defmodelcatdef1} The category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ of simplicial sets has a model structure in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is a weak homotopy equivalence;

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism; and

\item a fibration if and only if $f$ is a Kan fibration.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent The category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ of pointed simplicial sets also has a model structure with the same description.

\end{example}
\noindent The model categories ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ and ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ have the additional property of being cofibrantly generated. In practice, the property of being cofibrantly generated facilitates the detection of (acyclic) fibrations in a model category.

\begin{definition}[{\cite[Definition 11.1.2]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{defcofgenmcdef} A model category $\mathcal{M}$ is \emph{cofibrantly generated} if there exist

\begin{enumerate}

\item a set $I$ of morphisms in $\mathcal{M}$, called a \emph{generating set of cofibrations} for $\mathcal{M}$; and

\item a set $J$ of morphisms in $\mathcal{M}$, called a \emph{generating set of acyclic cofibrations} for $\mathcal{M}$,
\end{enumerate}
\noindent such that

\begin{enumerate}

\item both $I$ and $J$ permit the small object argument \cite[Definition 10.5.15]{Hirschhorn2003};

\item a morphism $p$ is an acyclic fibration if and only if $p$ has the right lifting property with respect to $I$; and

\item a morphism $p$ is a fibration if and only if $p$ has the right lifting property with respect to $J$.
\end{enumerate}

\end{definition}

\begin{example}[{\cite[Examples 11.1.6 and 11.1.7]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{defcofgenmcdef1} Both ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ and ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ are cofibrantly generated.

\begin{enumerate}

\item A generating set of cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ is the set of all boundary inclusions $$I=\left\{i_{n} \colon \partial\Delta[n] \rightarrow \Delta[n] \mid n \geq 0\right\}.$$
Attaching a disjoint basepoint yields a generating set of cofibrations $I_{+}$ for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$.

\item A generating set of acyclic cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ is the set of all horn inclusions $$J=\left\{j_{n, k} \colon \Lambda[n, k] \rightarrow \Delta[n] \mid n \geq 1 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\}.$$
Attaching a disjoint basepoint yields a generating set of acyclic cofibrations $J_{+}$ for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{example}
\noindent What is more, ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ and ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ belong in the following class of well-behaved model categories, which is originally due to J.H. Smith and is particularly expedient for our purposes of left Bousfield localization.

\begin{definition}[{\cite[Definition 2.1]{Dugger2001}}]\label{defcombmodelcatdef} A model category $\mathcal{M}$ is \emph{combinatorial} if $\mathcal{M}$ is cofibrantly generated and its underlying category is locally presentable \cite[Definition 1.17]{AdamekRosicky1994}.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[{\cite[Example 1.12]{AdamekRosicky1994}}]\label{defcombmodelcatdef1} Both ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ and ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ are locally presentable, thus combinatorial.
\end{example}
\noindent Lastly, ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ and ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ satisfy the following model-categorical property that permits left Bousfield localization.

\begin{definition}[{\cite[Definition 13.1.1(1)]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{deflpropmodelcatdef} A model category $\mathcal{M}$ is \emph{left proper} if every pushout of a weak equivalence along a cofibration is a weak equivalence.
\end{definition}
\noindent The following result guarantees the left properness of almost every model category that appears in this paper.

\begin{proposition}[{\cite[Corollary 13.1.3(1)]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{leftpropernoworries} If every object of a model category $\mathcal{M}$ is cofibrant, then $\mathcal{M}$ is left proper.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{The left transfer to $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section3.2}
\noindent Firstly, to appreciate the necessity of using pointed simplicial sets and their $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex, we explain why we cannot use the $1$-reduction functor $R_{1}$ to right-induce, in the sense of \cite[Theorem 11.3.2]{Hirschhorn2003}, a model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ along the adjunction $${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \colon U$$ from Lemma \ref{notquite}. We write $J$ for the set of all horn inclusions in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$. If such a right transfer were possible, then, in the resulting right-induced model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$,

\begin{enumerate}

\item the class of weak equivalences would be the class of weak homotopy equivalences; and

\item the set $R_{1}(J)$ would be a generating set of acyclic cofibrations.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Thus, every map in $R_{1}(J)$ would be a weak homotopy equivalence. We verify that this necessary condition fails.

\begin{lemma}\label{sadcarefree} For $n \geq 1$ and $0 \leq k \leq n$, let $j_{n, k} \colon \Lambda[n, k] \rightarrow \Delta[n]$ denote the horn inclusion in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$.

\begin{enumerate}

\item If $n=1$, then ${R}_{1}\left(j_{1, k}\right)$ is the identity map of the point.

\item If $n=2$, then ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right) \colon \Delta[0] \rightarrow S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}$ picks out the unique $0$-simplex of the simplicial $2$-sphere.

\item If $n \geq 3$, then $|{R}_{1}\left(j_{n, k}\right)|$ is the inclusion of a strong deformation retract in topological spaces.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Therefore, ${R}_{1}\left(j_{n, k}\right)$ is a weak homotopy equivalence if and only if $n \neq 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} Parts (1) and (2) are straightforward. For (3), if $n \geq 3$, then $\operatorname{sk}_{1}\left(\Lambda[n, k]\right)=\operatorname{sk}_{1}\left(\Delta[n]\right)$, so the inclusion of a strong deformation retract $|j_{n, k}|$ descends to the inclusion of a strong deformation retract $|{R}_{1}\left(j_{n, k}\right)|$.
\end{proof}
\noindent On that note, we also record a computation of a similar nature for future reference in this section.

\begin{lemma}\label{notallislost} If a simplicial map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is a monomorphism, then so is its $1$-reduction ${R}_{1}(f)$.
\end{lemma}
\noindent We left-induce a model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ by transferring the model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ along the adjunction $$F \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \colon E_{1}$$ from Lemma \ref{yesquite}(2). To do so, we apply the following general result on left-induced model structures.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 2.23]{BHKKRS2015}}]\label{leftinducetheorem} Let $F \colon \mathcal{C} \rightleftarrows \mathcal{M} \colon G$ be an adjunction, where $\mathcal{C}$ is a locally presentable category and $\mathcal{M}$ is a combinatorial model category with class of cofibrations $Q$. Suppose that the following condition holds: if a morphism $g$ in $\mathcal{C}$ has the right lifting property with respect to the class $F^{-1}(Q)$, then $F(g)$ is a weak equivalence in $\mathcal{M}$. Then, there is a combinatorial model structure on $\mathcal{C}$ in which a morphism $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $F(f)$ is a weak equivalence in $\mathcal{M}$; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $F(f)$ is a cofibration in $\mathcal{M}$.

\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\noindent As ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is a locally presentable category \cite[Example 1.12]{AdamekRosicky1994} and ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ is a combinatorial model category by Example \ref{defcombmodelcatdef1}, it remains to verify the condition in Theorem \ref{leftinducetheorem}.

\begin{lemma}\label{verifybhkkrs} Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. If $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to every monomorphism of $1$-reduced simplicial sets, then $f$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} We show that $f$ is an acyclic fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$. For $n \geq 0$, let $i_{n} \colon \partial\Delta[n] \rightarrow \Delta[n]$ denote the boundary inclusion in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$. By Lemma \ref{notallislost}, ${R}_{1}(i_{n})$ is a monomorphism of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. Therefore, the hypothesis informs us that, for every $n \geq 0$, $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}(i_{n})$. Then, the adjunction ${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \colon U$ from Lemma \ref{notquite} yields that, for every $n \geq 0$, $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to the boundary inclusion $i_{n}$. We conclude that $f$ is an acyclic fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$.
\end{proof}
\noindent We begin our recovery of the family of model structures on $1$-reduced simplicial sets in \cite[Part II, Theorem 2.2]{Quillen1969} and the characterization of fibrant objects in \cite[Part II, Corollary 2.6]{Quillen1969}.

\begin{theorem}\label{rightinduceeyebrow} There is a combinatorial and left proper model structure on the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ of $1$-reduced simplicial sets in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is a weak homotopy equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent In this model structure, a $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is fibrant if and only if $X$ is a Kan complex.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof} Theorem \ref{leftinducetheorem} yields every claim other than the characterization of fibrant objects. Every $1$-reduced Kan complex $X$ is fibrant in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ because, by virtue of being a Kan complex, $X$ has the right lifting property with respect to the class of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. Conversely, if a $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is fibrant in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, then we show that $X$ is a Kan complex. For $n \geq 1$ and $0 \leq k \leq n$, we solve a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Lambda[n, k]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n].}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{j_{n, k}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent Using the adjunction ${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \colon U$ from Lemma \ref{notquite}, we may equivalently solve a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{{R}_{1}\left(\Lambda[n, k]\right)} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{{R}_{1}\left(\Delta[n]\right).}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{n, k}\right)}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent In light of Lemma \ref{sadcarefree}, we split our solution in two separate cases. If $n \neq 2$, then, by Lemmas \ref{sadcarefree} and \ref{notallislost}, ${R}_{1}\left(j_{n, k}\right)$ is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, so our lifting problem has a solution since $X$ is fibrant in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. If $n=2$, then we solve a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Delta[0]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2},}
	\arrow["{x_{0}}", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{\psi}", dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where $x_{0}$ denotes the unique $0$-simplex of $X$, by setting $\psi$ to be the constant map at $x_{0}$.
\end{proof}
\noindent The left transfer in Theorem \ref{rightinduceeyebrow} renders the adjunction $$F \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \colon E_{1}$$ from Lemma \ref{yesquite}(2) a Quillen adjunction; in particular, we note that $E_{1}$ preserves fibrant objects.

\begin{corollary}\label{forlaterusetba} If $X$ is a pointed Kan complex, then $E_{1}(X)$ is a Kan complex.
\end{corollary}
\noindent In particular, we occasionally appeal to a special instance of Corollary \ref{forlaterusetba}.

\begin{corollary}\label{forlaterusetbacor} If $Y$ is a pointed topological space, then $E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Y)$ is a Kan complex.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof} By Example \ref{defkanfibrdef2}, $\operatorname{Sing}(Y)$ is a pointed Kan complex, so the claim follows from Corollary \ref{forlaterusetba}.
\end{proof}

\section{Fibrations of $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section4}
\noindent We study the fibrations in the model category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. Namely, we produce functorial fibrant replacements, a generating set of cofibrations, and a set of acyclic cofibrations that detects fibrant objects. Moreover, we characterize when a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is a Kan fibration, and we find a counterintuitive fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\subsection{Fibrant replacement of $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section4.1} We invoke Corollary \ref{forlaterusetbacor} to produce functorial fibrant replacements in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. Let $X$ be a $1$-reduced simplicial set. The geometric realization-singular complex adjunction from Proposition \ref{actuallysimpadj} provides the natural map $$X \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(|X|),$$ which maps into the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex $E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(|X|)$. Thus, we obtain the commutative diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	X \&\& {E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(|X|)} \\
	\\
	\&\& {\operatorname{Sing}(|X|),}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where $E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(|X|) \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(|X|)$ is the inclusion map. We show that the natural map $$X \rightarrow E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(|X|)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence, thus a functorial fibrant replacement of $X$ in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. To do so, it suffices to show that the other two maps in the commutative diagram are weak homotopy equivalences. Firstly, Part (1) of the following theorem informs us that the natural map $$X \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(|X|)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence; Part (2) is for later use in this argument.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Chapter I, Theorem 11.4]{GoerssJardine1999}}]\label{quilleneqsecretly}

\begin{enumerate}

\item For every simplicial set $X$, the natural map $$X \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(|X|)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item For every topological space $Y$, the natural map $$|\operatorname{Sing}(Y)| \rightarrow Y$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\end{enumerate}

\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\label{quilleneqsecretlyfibreprmk} Part (1) informs us that, for a simplicial set $X$, the natural map $$X \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(|X|)$$ is a functorial fibrant replacement of $X$ in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$, as $\operatorname{Sing}(|X|)$ is a Kan complex by Example \ref{defkanfibrdef2}.
\end{remark}
\noindent As for the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex inclusion $$E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(|X|) \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(|X|),$$ it is also a weak homotopy equivalence by a special case of the following theorem.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 8.4(iii)]{May1967}}]\label{mo67} If $X$ is a pointed Kan complex, then the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex inclusion $E_{1}(X) \rightarrow X$ induces, for every $n \geq 2$, the isomorphism $$\pi_{n}\left(E_{1}(X)\right) \xrightarrow{\cong} \pi_{n}(X).$$
In particular, if $X$ is a pointed and $1$-connected Kan complex, then the inclusion $$E_{1}(X) \rightarrow X$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\label{mo67rmk} In other words, if $X$ is a pointed Kan complex, then the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex inclusion $$E_{1}(X) \rightarrow X$$ is a $1$-connected cover of $X$.
\end{remark}
\noindent As an aside, using the construction of the homotopy groups of Kan complexes in \cite[Chapter I.7]{GoerssJardine1999} together with Lemma \ref{unproprEilenberg}, we can provide an alternative proof of Theorem \ref{mo67} from that in {\cite[Theorem 8.4(iii)]{May1967}}.

\begin{proof} Let $X$ be a pointed Kan complex. Let $n \geq 2$. We first show that $\pi_{n}\left(E_{1}(X)\right) \rightarrow \pi_{n}(X)$ is an epimorphism. As $X$ is a Kan complex, every element of $\pi_{n}(X)$ is of the form $[\omega]$ for a map $$\omega \colon S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n} \rightarrow X.$$ As $n \geq 2$, the simplicial $n$-sphere $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$ is $1$-reduced, so the universal property of the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex from Lemma \ref{unproprEilenberg} yields the commutative diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}} \&\& {E_{1}(X)} \\
	\\
	\&\& {X.}
	\arrow["{\hat{\omega}}", dashed, from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{\omega}"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent It remains to show that $\pi_{n}\left(E_{1}(X)\right) \rightarrow \pi_{n}(X)$ is a monomorphism. Given a pointed homotopy $$H \colon S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n} \wedge \Delta[1]_{+} \rightarrow X,$$ we observe that $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n} \wedge \Delta[1]_{+}$ is $1$-reduced by Lemma \ref{smashingtensor} because $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$ is $1$-reduced, so the universal property of the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex from Lemma \ref{unproprEilenberg} yields the commutative diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n} \wedge \Delta[1]_{+}} \&\& {E_{1}(X)} \\
	\\
	\&\& {X.}
	\arrow["{\hat{H}}", dashed, from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{H}"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{mo67cor} If $Y$ is a pointed and $1$-connected topological space, then the inclusion $$E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Y) \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(Y)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof} By Example \ref{defkanfibrdef2}, $\operatorname{Sing}(Y)$ is a Kan complex. By Theorem \ref{quilleneqsecretly}(2), $\operatorname{Sing}(Y)$ is $1$-connected.
\end{proof}
\noindent In conclusion, we obtain functorial fibrant replacements in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\begin{proposition}\label{fibrepinogcat} Let $X$ be a $1$-reduced simplicial set. Then, the natural map $$X \rightarrow E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(|X|)$$ is a functorial fibrant replacement of $X$ in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Generating sets of cofibrations of $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section4.2}
\noindent Our left transfer from Theorem \ref{rightinduceeyebrow} provides us with a combinatorial model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, but not with explicit generating sets of cofibrations and acyclic cofibrations. As a first remedy, we produce an explicit generating set of cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\begin{proposition}\label{gencofset} Let $I=\left\{i_{n} \colon \partial\Delta[n] \rightarrow \Delta[n] \mid n \geq 0\right\}$ denote the generating set of cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$. Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. The following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is an acyclic fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$.

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is an acyclic fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ has the right lifting property with respect to the set $${R}_{1}(I)=\left\{{R}_{1}(i_{n}) \colon {R}_{1}(\partial\Delta[n]) \rightarrow {R}_{1}(\Delta[n]) \mid n \geq 0\right\}.$$

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Hence, ${R}_{1}(I)$ is a generating set of cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Firstly, we show that (1) implies (2). If $f$ is an acyclic fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$, then $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to all monomorphisms in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$. In particular, $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to all monomorphisms in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, so $f$ is an acyclic fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. \\ \\
Then, (2) implies (3) as, by Lemma \ref{notallislost}, ${R}_{1}(I)$ is a set of cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. \\ \\
Lastly, we show that (3) implies (1). Suppose $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}(I)$. To prove that $f$ is an acyclic fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$, we solve, for $n \geq 0$, a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\partial\Delta[n]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n]} \&\& {Y.}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{i_{n}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent Under the adjunction ${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \colon U$ from Lemma \ref{notquite}, we equivalently solve a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{{R}_{1}(\partial\Delta[n])} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{{R}_{1}(\Delta[n])} \&\& {Y.}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}(i_{n})}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent As ${R}_{1}(i_{n})$ is in ${R}_{1}(I)$ and $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}(I)$, a solution exists.
\end{proof}
\noindent Even though our treatment of Proposition \ref{gencofset} is biased towards the equivalence of (2) and (3), which grants us our generating set of cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, we would be remiss if we neglected the equivalence of (1) and (2). The fact that (1) and (2) are equivalent means that ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ has no pathological acyclic fibrations. As we shall see later in this section, Proposition \ref{gencofset} finds no analog in the study of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. The reason is that ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ has fibrations that are dissimilar to Kan fibrations, a phenomenon that we also investigate in this section. Thus, the model category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is more than a restriction of the model category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ to $1$-reduced simplicial sets; after all, the model category axioms are generally not amenable to such a restriction. Instead, the model category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ formalizes a different homotopy theory that is interesting in its own right.

\subsection{Acyclic cofibrations of $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section4.3}
\noindent Our attempt to emulate Proposition \ref{gencofset} towards a generating set of acyclic cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ encounters a problem: the $1$-reduction of a boundary inclusion is a cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, but the $1$-reduction of a horn inclusion need not be an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\begin{lemma}\label{combinebadnews} For $n \geq 1$ and $0 \leq k \leq n$, let $j_{n, k} \colon \Lambda[n, k] \rightarrow \Delta[n]$ denote the horn inclusion in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$. Then, ${R}_{1}\left(j_{n, k}\right)$ is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ if and only if $n \neq 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} The claim follows from Lemmas \ref{sadcarefree} and \ref{notallislost} and the definition of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.
\end{proof}
\noindent Nevertheless, we can partially work our way around this obstacle. Let $$J=\left\{j_{n, k} \colon \Lambda[n, k] \rightarrow \Delta[n] \mid n \geq 1 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\}$$ denote the generating set of acyclic cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$. Write $${R}_{1}(J)=\left\{{R}_{1}(j_{n, k}) \colon {R}_{1}(\Lambda[n, k]) \rightarrow {R}_{1}(\Delta[n]) \mid n \geq 1 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\},$$ which is a set of cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ by Lemma \ref{notallislost}, but not a set of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ by Lemma \ref{combinebadnews}. To extract a candidate generating set of acyclic cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$, we use Lemma \ref{sadcarefree} to strike out

\begin{enumerate}

\item the maps ${R}_{1}(j_{1, 0})$ and ${R}_{1}(j_{1, 1})$, which are the identity map of the point; and

\item the maps ${R}_{1}(j_{2, 0})$, ${R}_{1}(j_{2, 1})$, and ${R}_{1}(j_{2, 2})$ which are not weak homotopy equivalences.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Therefore, we write $$\widetilde{J}=\left\{j_{n, k} \colon \Lambda[n, k] \rightarrow \Delta[n] \mid n \geq 3 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\},$$ whose elements are acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$, in order to write $${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})=\left\{{R}_{1}(j_{n, k}) \colon {R}_{1}(\Lambda[n, k]) \rightarrow {R}_{1}(\Delta[n]) \mid n \geq 3 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\},$$ which is a set of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. Hence, having the right lifting property with respect to the set ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$ is necessary for a map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets to be a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. We partially address the question of sufficiency. Namely, we establish that the set ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$ detects fibrant objects in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\begin{proposition}\label{quasicataddendum} Let $X$ be a $1$-reduced simplicial set. The following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex.

\item The $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is fibrant in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\item The unique map $c \colon X \rightarrow \Delta[0]$ has the right lifting property with respect to the set $${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})=\left\{{R}_{1}(j_{n, k}) \colon {R}_{1}(\Lambda[n, k]) \rightarrow {R}_{1}(\Delta[n]) \mid n \geq 3 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\}.$$

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Hence, the set ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$ detects fibrant objects in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Firstly, (1) and (2) are equivalent by the characterization of fibrant objects in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ in Theorem \ref{rightinduceeyebrow}. \\ \\
If (2) holds, then $c \colon X \rightarrow \Delta[0]$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. Thus, $c \colon X \rightarrow \Delta[0]$ has the right lifting property with respect to the set ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$, since every map in ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$ is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. Therefore, (3) holds. \\ \\
Lastly, suppose (3) holds. We prove that (1) holds. For $n \geq 1$ and $0 \leq k \leq n$, a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Lambda[n, k]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n]}
	\arrow["h", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{j_{n, k}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["g", dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent is equivalent, under the adjunction ${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \colon U$ from Lemma \ref{notquite}, to a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{{R}_{1}\left(\Lambda[n, k]\right)} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{{R}_{1}\left(\Delta[n]\right).}
	\arrow["\eta", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{n, k}\right)}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["\psi", dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent In light of Lemma \ref{sadcarefree}, we split our solution in three separate cases. If $n=1$, then Lemma \ref{sadcarefree}(1) says that ${R}_{1}\left(j_{1, k}\right)$ is the identity map of the point, so our lifting problem can be solved by setting $\psi=\eta$. If $n \geq 3$, then our lifting problem can be solved, as we are assuming that $c \colon X \rightarrow \Delta[0]$ has the right lifting property with respect to the set ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$. Lastly, if $n=2$, then Lemma \ref{sadcarefree}(2) says that our lifting problem is
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Delta[0]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2},}
	\arrow["{x_{0}}", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["\psi", dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where $x_{0}$ denotes the unique $0$-simplex of $X$, so we take $\psi$ to be the constant map at $x_{0}$.
\end{proof}
\noindent In fact, a different and more elaborate argument yields a stronger result.

\begin{proposition}[{\cite[Part II, Proposition 2.12]{Quillen1969}}]\label{quillendidit} Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. Suppose that $Y$ is a Kan complex. The following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ has the right lifting property with respect to the set $${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})=\left\{{R}_{1}(j_{n, k}) \colon {R}_{1}(\Lambda[n, k]) \rightarrow {R}_{1}(\Delta[n]) \mid n \geq 3 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\}.$$

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Hence, the set ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$ detects fibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ whose target is a Kan complex.

\end{proposition}
\noindent The additional fibrancy condition on the target is crucially used in Quillen's proof, which suggests that ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$ is not a generating set of acyclic cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. In his discussion of \cite[Part II, Proposition 2.12]{Quillen1969}, Quillen affirms this suggestion and provides a hint towards showing that the fibrancy condition on the target in Proposition \ref{quillendidit} is necessary. However, to the best of our knowledge, the question of whether ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$ is a generating set of acyclic cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ remains open. Our various attempts at producing a map in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ that has the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$ but is not a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ have been unsuccessful in the same fashion: our candidate maps are not fibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, but they also fail to have the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})$. We pose the following conjecture to the community.

\begin{conjecture}\label{nooneknows} Let ${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightarrow {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ denote the $1$-reduction functor. The set $${R}_{1}(\widetilde{J})=\left\{{R}_{1}(j_{n, k}) \colon {R}_{1}(\Lambda[n, k]) \rightarrow {R}_{1}(\Delta[n]) \mid n \geq 3 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\}$$ is not a generating set of acyclic cofibrations for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.
\end{conjecture}

\subsection{Fibrations of $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section4.4}
\noindent The issues that we ran into in our study of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ suggest that there exist fibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ that are dissimilar to Kan fibrations. To investigate this phenomenon, we characterize when a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is a Kan fibration. In fact, we produce two results to that end, which help us find an explicit example of a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ that is not a Kan fibration. \\ \\
To begin with, we note that every Kan fibration of $1$-reduced simplicial sets is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\begin{proposition}\label{kanimpliesfibssetr} If $f$ is a Kan fibration of $1$-reduced simplicial sets, then $f$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} If $f$ is a Kan fibration, then $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to the class of acyclic cofibrations in $\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$, which contains the class of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. Thus, $f$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.
\end{proof}
\noindent We prove a characterization that detects when a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is a Kan fibration and helps us disprove the converse of Proposition \ref{kanimpliesfibssetr}. Our result is the $1$-reduced analog of \cite[Lemma 6.6]{GoerssJardine1999}. Recall from Lemma \ref{sadcarefree} that, for $0 \leq k \leq 2$, the map ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right) \colon \Delta[0] \rightarrow S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}$ picks the unique $0$-simplex of the simplicial $2$-sphere.

\begin{proposition}\label{litmus} Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. For $0 \leq k \leq 2$, let $j_{2, k} \colon \Lambda[2, k] \rightarrow \Delta[2]$ denote the horn inclusion. The following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is a Kan fibration.

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ has the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)$ for $0 \leq k \leq 2$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Under the adjunction ${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \colon U$ from Lemma \ref{notquite}, a lifting problem of the type
\begin{equation}\tag{I}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Delta[0]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}} \&\& {Y,}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
\noindent is equivalent to a lifting problem of the type
\begin{equation}\tag{II}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Lambda[2, k]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{\Delta[2]} \&\& {Y.}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{j_{2, k}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
\noindent If $f$ is a Kan fibration, then every lifting problem of type (II) has a solution. Thus, every lifting problem of type (I) has a solution, and $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)$ for $0 \leq k \leq 2$. \\ \\
Conversely, if $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)$ for $0 \leq k \leq 2$, then every lifting problem of type (II) has a solution. If $n \neq 2$ and $0 \leq t \leq n$, then solving a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Lambda[n, t]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n]} \&\& {Y}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{j_{n, t}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent is equivalent, under the adjunction ${R}_{1} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \colon U$ from Lemma \ref{notquite}, to solving a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{{R}_{1}\left(\Lambda[n, t]\right)} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{{R}_{1}\left(\Delta[n]\right)} \&\& {Y.}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{n, t}\right)}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent By Lemma \ref{combinebadnews}, ${R}_{1}\left(j_{n, t}\right)$ is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ because $n \neq 2$, so our lifting problem has a solution because $f$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. Overall, we conclude that $f$ is a Kan fibration.
\end{proof}
\noindent Proposition \ref{litmus} helps us find a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ that bears no resemblance to a Kan fibration. To do so, we need an auxiliary lemma about maps from a $1$-reduced simplicial set to the simplicial $2$-sphere $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}$.

\begin{lemma}\label{nocontradiction} Let $B$ be a $1$-reduced simplicial set. Then, a map $h \colon B \rightarrow S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}$ is constant if and only if $$h_{\ast} \colon \pi_{2}(B) \rightarrow \pi_{2}(S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2})$$ is not an epimorphism.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} If $h$ is constant, then so is $h_{\ast}$. If $h$ is not constant, then there is a $2$-simplex $\omega$ of $B$ such that $h(\omega)$ is the unique non-degenerate $2$-simplex $\epsilon$ of $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}$. As $B$ is $1$-reduced, Corollary \ref{notquitesimplices} allows us to view $\omega$ as a map $$\omega \colon S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2} \rightarrow B.$$ Then, we compute that $$h_{\ast}\left([\omega]\right)=[h(\omega)]=[\epsilon],$$
which is a generator of $\pi_{2}(S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}) \cong \mathbb{Z}$. Thus, $h_{\ast}$ is an epimorphism.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{litmuscor} For $0 \leq k \leq 2$, the map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right) \colon \Delta[0] \rightarrow S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ but not a Kan fibration.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} We first show that ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. For an acyclic cofibration $\gamma \colon A \rightarrow B$ in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ and a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	A \&\&\& {\Delta[0]} \\
	\\
	B \&\&\& {S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2},}
	\arrow["c", from=1-1, to=1-4]
	\arrow["\gamma"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)}", from=1-4, to=3-4]
	\arrow["g", dashed, from=3-1, to=1-4]
	\arrow["h", from=3-1, to=3-4]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent we claim that the unique option for $g$ solves our lifting problem. The upper left triangle has to commute. To show that the lower right triangle also commutes, we show that $h$ is constant. By Lemma \ref{nocontradiction}, we show that $$h_{\ast} \colon \pi_{2}(B) \rightarrow \pi_{2}(S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2})$$ is not an epimorphism; in fact, we show that $h_{\ast}$ is constant. Firstly, as $c_{\ast}$ is constant, so is $$h_{\ast}\gamma_{\ast}=(h\gamma)_{\ast}=({{R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)}c)_{\ast}={{R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)}_{\ast}c_{\ast}.$$ Because $\gamma$ is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, we know that $\gamma_{\ast}$ is an isomorphism. Therefore, $h_{\ast}$ is constant. \\ \\
Now, we apply the characterization from Proposition \ref{litmus} to argue that ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)$ is not a Kan fibration because it does not have the right lifting property with respect to itself. Indeed, the lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Delta[0]} \&\&\& {\Delta[0]} \\
	\\
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}} \&\&\& {S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}}
	\arrow[Rightarrow, no head, from=1-1, to=1-4]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)}", from=1-4, to=3-4]
	\arrow["g", dashed, from=3-1, to=1-4]
	\arrow[Rightarrow, no head, from=3-1, to=3-4]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent has no solution because the unique option for $g$ does not make the lower right triangle commute.
\end{proof}
\noindent In the presence of a fibrancy condition on the target, Proposition \ref{litmus} can be strengthened. Namely, we establish the following characterization result, which is the $1$-reduced analog of \cite[Corollary 6.9]{GoerssJardine1999}.

\begin{proposition}\label{litmussuper} Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. Suppose that $Y$ is a Kan complex. Then, $X$ is also a Kan complex, and the following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is a Kan fibration.

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ has the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)$ for $0 \leq k \leq 2$.

\item The map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ induces an epimorphism $f_{\ast} \colon \pi_{2}(X) \rightarrow \pi_{2}(Y)$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{proposition}
\noindent In our proof, we use the construction of the homotopy groups of Kan complexes from \cite[Chapter I.7]{GoerssJardine1999}.

\begin{proof} The unique map $X \rightarrow \Delta[0]$ factors in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ as the composite of fibrations $X \xrightarrow{f} Y \rightarrow \Delta[0]$. Hence, $X$ is fibrant in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, thus a Kan complex by the characterization of fibrant objects in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ from Theorem \ref{rightinduceeyebrow}. \\ \\
Now, Proposition \ref{litmus} says that (1) and (2) are equivalent. We show that (2) and (3) are equivalent. Firstly, suppose that $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to ${R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)$ for $0 \leq k \leq 2$. As $Y$ is a Kan complex, every element of $\pi_{2}(Y)$ is of the form $[\omega]$ for a map $$\omega \colon S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2} \rightarrow Y.$$ We solve the lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Delta[0]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}} \&\& Y
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["\alpha", dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["\omega", from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent to produce an element $[\alpha] \in \pi_{2}(X)$ such that $$f_{\ast}([\alpha])=[f\alpha]=[\omega] \in \pi_{2}(Y).$$
Conversely, suppose that $f$ induces an epimorphism at $\pi_{2}$. For $0 \leq k \leq 2$, we solve a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Delta[0]} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}} \&\& {Y.}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{{R}_{1}\left(j_{2, k}\right)}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["\psi", dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["\eta", from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent As $f_{\ast}$ is an epimorphism, there is a commutative diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	\&\& {S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}} \&\& {S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2} \wedge \Delta[1]_{+}} \&\& {Y,}
	\arrow["\beta", from=1-3, to=1-5]
	\arrow["{d^{1}}"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-5, to=3-5]
	\arrow["{d^{0}}", from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["\eta"', curve={height=24pt}, from=3-1, to=3-5]
	\arrow["H", from=3-3, to=3-5]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where $$f_{\ast}([\beta])=[f\beta]=[\eta] \in \pi_{2}(Y)$$ by means of the pointed homotopy $$H \colon S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2} \wedge \Delta[1]_{+} \rightarrow Y$$ starting from $Hd^{1}=f\beta$ and ending at $Hd^{0}=\eta$. By Lemma \ref{smashingtensor}, $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2} \wedge \Delta[1]_{+}$ is $1$-reduced. As $d^{1}$ is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ and $f$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, there exists a lift
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	\&\& {S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2}} \&\& {S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{2} \wedge \Delta[1]_{+}} \&\& {Y.}
	\arrow["\beta", from=1-3, to=1-5]
	\arrow["{d^{1}}"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-5, to=3-5]
	\arrow["{d^{0}}", from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["\eta"', curve={height=24pt}, from=3-1, to=3-5]
	\arrow["g", dashed, from=3-3, to=1-5]
	\arrow["H", from=3-3, to=3-5]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent Then, $\psi=gd^{0}$ solves our lifting problem because $$f\psi=fgd^{0}=Hd^{0}=\eta.$$
\end{proof}
\noindent The fibrancy condition in Proposition \ref{litmussuper} is the reason why we could not have used it for our example of a pathological fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ from Proposition \ref{litmuscor}. Indeed, we conclude this section by showing that, for $n \geq 2$, the simplicial $n$-sphere $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$ is not a Kan complex. To that end, we need a counting lemma.

\begin{lemma}\label{countingfaces} Let $x$ be an $n$-simplex of a simplicial set. For $0 \leq k \leq n$, let $s_{k}(x)$ be the $k$-th degeneracy of $x$.

\begin{enumerate}

\item If $x$ is a non-degenerate $n$-simplex, then $s_{k}(x)$ has exactly $2$ non-degenerate faces.

\item If $x$ is a degenerate $n$-simplex, then $s_{k}(x)$ has no non-degenerate faces.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Hence, in either case, $s_{k}(x)$ has at most $2$ non-degenerate faces.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} For $0 \leq i \leq n+1$, let $d_{i}s_{k}(x)$ be the $i$-th face of $s_{k}(x)$. The result follows from the simplicial identities: $$d_{i}s_{k}(x) = \begin{cases} s_{k-1}d_{i}(x), \textrm{ if } 0 \leq i \leq k-1; \\
x, \textrm{ if } k \leq i \leq k+1; \\
s_{k}d_{i-1}(x), \textrm{ if } k+2 \leq i \leq n+1. \end{cases}$$
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{srkannot} For $n \geq 2$, the simplicial $n$-sphere $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$ is not a Kan complex, thus not fibrant in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Write $\epsilon$ for the unique non-degenerate $n$-simplex of $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$. For $0 \leq k \leq n+1$, consider the lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Lambda[n+1, k]} \&\& {S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}} \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n+1],}
	\arrow["h", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{j_{n+1, k}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["g", dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where $h$ is the horn that picks out the $n$-simplices $\left(\epsilon, \dots, \epsilon\right)$. Any simplex $g$ that fills in this horn would be a degenerate $(n+1)$-simplex with $n+1$ non-degenerate faces, which is impossible for $n \geq 2$ by Lemma \ref{countingfaces}.
\end{proof}

\section{The simplicial model structure on $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section5}
\noindent A convenient feature of the model categories ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ and ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ is that they are simplicial, so they carry more structure for us to use. This feature motivates us to endow ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ with a simplicial model structure. We modify the simplicial model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ to define function complexes, tensors, and cotensors of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. Then, we verify that our definitions endow ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ with a simplicial model structure.

\subsection{Simplicial model categories}\label{section5.1}
\noindent A reference on simplicial model categories is \cite[Chapter 9]{Hirschhorn2003}.

\begin{definition}[{\cite[Definition 9.1.2]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{defsmcdef} Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a model category that is enriched over simplicial sets; in particular, for two objects $X$ and $Y$ of $\mathcal{M}$, we have a \emph{function complex} $\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(X, Y\right)$. Then, $\mathcal{M}$ is a \emph{simplicial model category} if $\mathcal{M}$ satisfies the following axioms.

\begin{enumerate}

\item (\emph{Adjointness axiom}) For two objects $X$ and $Y$ of $\mathcal{M}$ and a simplicial set $Z$, there is a \emph{tensor} object $X \otimes Z$ of $\mathcal{M}$ and a \emph{cotensor} object $Y^{Z}$ of $\mathcal{M}$ such that there are isomorphisms of simplicial sets
$$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(X \otimes Z, Y\right) \cong \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}}\left(Z, \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(X, Y\right)\right) \cong \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(X, Y^{Z}\right)$$
that are natural in $X$, $Y$, and $Z$. Note that $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}}\left(Z, \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(X, Y\right)\right)$ is a function complex of simplicial sets; see Example \ref{defsmcdef1}.

\item (\emph{Homotopy lifting-extension axiom}) If $i \colon A \rightarrow B$ is a cofibration in $\mathcal{M}$ and $p \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is a fibration in $\mathcal{M}$, then the induced map
$$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(B, X\right) \xrightarrow{(i^{\ast}, p_{\ast})} \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(A, X\right) \underset{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(A, Y\right)}{\times} \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(B, Y\right)$$
is a Kan fibration, which is also a weak homotopy equivalence if $i$ or $p$ is also a weak equivalence in $\mathcal{M}$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{definition}

\begin{example}[{\cite[Example 9.1.13]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{defsmcdef1} The category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ is a simplicial model category. Given simplicial sets $X$, $Y$, and $Z$, we define

\begin{enumerate}

\item the function complex $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}}\left(X, Y\right)$ to be the simplicial set with $n$-simplices $\operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}}\left(X \times \Delta[n], Y\right)$;

\item the tensor of $X$ with $Z$ to be the simplicial set $X \times Z$; and

\item the cotensor of $X$ with $Z$ to be the simplicial set $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}}\left(Z, X\right)$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{example}

\begin{example}[{\cite[Example 9.1.14]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{defsmcdef2} The category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ is a simplicial model category. For two pointed simplicial sets $X$ and $Y$ and a simplicial set $Z$, we define

\begin{enumerate}

\item the function complex $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X, Y\right)$ as the simplicial set with $n$-simplices $\operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}, Y\right)$;

\item the tensor of $X$ with $Z$ to be the pointed simplicial set $X \times Z_{+}$; and

\item the cotensor of $X$ with $Z$ to be the pointed simplicial set $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(Z_{+}, X\right)$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{example}

\subsection{Function complexes, tensors, and cotensors of $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section5.2} We modify the simplicial model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ to define function complexes, tensors, and cotensors in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. We define

\begin{enumerate}

\item the function complex of two $1$-reduced simplicial sets $X$ and $Y$ to be $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)$;

\item the tensor of a $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ with a simplicial set $Z$ to be $X \wedge Z_{+}$; and

\item the cotensor of a $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ with a simplicial set $Z$ to be $E_{1}\left(\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(Z_{+}, X)\right)$.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection{An exponential law}\label{section5.3} The simplicial model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ is underpinned by the exponential law for simplicial sets \cite[Chapter I, Proposition 5.1]{GoerssJardine1999}. We formulate an exponential law for pointed simplicial sets for our simplicial model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. For two pointed simplicial sets $X$ and $Y$, the \emph{evaluation} $$\operatorname{ev} \colon X \wedge \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y) \rightarrow Y$$ is defined at the level of $n$-simplices by $$\operatorname{ev}_{n}([x, f])=f_{n}([x, \sigma_{n}]),$$ where $\sigma_{n}$ is the unique non-degenerate $n$-simplex of $\Delta[n]$. We establish the following exponential law.

\begin{proposition}\label{pointedexplaw} For pointed simplicial sets $X$, $Y$, and $Z$, the map $$\operatorname{ev}_{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(Z, \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X \wedge Z, Y\right)$$ sending $f \colon Z \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)$ to the composite $$X \wedge Z \xrightarrow{\operatorname{id}_{X} \wedge f} X \wedge \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y) \xrightarrow{\operatorname{ev}} Y$$ is a bijection that is natural in $X$, $Y$, and $Z$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}\label{r8} This natural bijection amounts to the adjunction $X \wedge - \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, -)$.
\end{remark}

\begin{proof} The inverse of $\operatorname{ev}_{\ast}$ sends a map $g \colon X \wedge Z \rightarrow Y$ to the map $$\hat{g} \colon Z \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)$$ defined by sending an $n$-simplex $z$ of $Z$, which we view as a map $z \colon \Delta[n] \rightarrow Z$, to the composite $$X \wedge \Delta[n]_{+} \xrightarrow{\operatorname{id}_{X} \wedge \widetilde{z}} A \wedge Z \xrightarrow{g} Y,$$ where, since $Z$ is pointed, we use Lemma \ref{pointtheunpointed} to induce the pointed map $\widetilde{z} \colon \Delta[n]_{+} \rightarrow Z$.
\end{proof}
\noindent In particular, we use the following consequence of our exponential law.

\begin{corollary}\label{pointedexplawcor} For a $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$, there is an adjunction $$X \wedge - \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}\colon \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, -).$$
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof} By Lemma \ref{smashingtensor}, the functor $X \wedge -$ maps into ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. The adjunction now follows from Remark \ref{r8}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Verification of the adjointness axiom}\label{section5.4} We use our exponential law to verify that the structure defined in Section \ref{section5.2} satisfies the adjointness axiom. We need an auxiliary lemma that is straightforward to check.

\begin{lemma}\label{smashanddisjoint} Given two simplicial sets $Z$ and $W$, there is an isomorphism of pointed simplicial sets $$\left(Z_{+}\right) \wedge \left(W_{+}\right) \cong \left(Z \times W\right)_{+}$$ that is natural in $Z$ and $W$.
\end{lemma}
\noindent We are ready to verify the adjointness axiom for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\begin{proposition}\label{simplicialaxiomm6} Given two $1$-reduced simplicial sets $X$ and $Y$ and a simplicial set $Z$, there are isomorphisms of simplicial sets
$$\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X \wedge Z_{+}, Y\right) \cong \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}}\left(Z, \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)\right) \cong \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X, E\left(\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(Z_{+}, Y)\right)\right)$$
that are natural in $X$, $Y$, and $Z$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} To prove $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X \wedge Z_{+}, Y\right) \cong \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}}\left(Z, \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)\right)$, we compute for arbitrary $n$ that

\begin{flalign*}
\operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(\left(X \wedge Z_{+}\right) \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}, Y\right) &\cong \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X \wedge \l\left(Z_{+} \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}\right), Y\right) \\
 &\cong \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(Z_{+} \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}, \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)\right) \\
 &\cong \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(\left(Z \times \Delta[n]\right)_{+}, \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)\right) \\
 &\cong \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}}\left(Z \times \Delta[n], \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)\right).
\end{flalign*}
\noindent The second bijection uses Proposition \ref{pointedexplaw}, the third one uses Lemma \ref{smashanddisjoint}, and the fourth one uses Lemma \ref{pointtheunpointed}. \\ \\
To prove $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X \wedge Z_{+}, Y\right) \cong \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X, E\left(\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(Z_{+}, Y)\right)\right)$, we compute for arbitrary $n$ that

\begin{flalign*}
\operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(\left(X \wedge Z_{+}\right) \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}, Y\right) &\cong \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(Z_{+} \wedge \left(X \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}\right), Y\right) \\
 &\cong \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}, \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(Z_{+}, Y)\right) \\
 &\cong \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}}\left(X \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}, E\left(\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(Z_{+}, Y)\right)\right) \\
 &= \operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(X \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}, E\left(\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(Z_{+}, Y)\right)\right).
\end{flalign*}
\noindent The second bijection comes from Proposition \ref{pointedexplaw}, and the third bijection is a consequence of Lemma \ref{yesquite}(2).
\end{proof}

\subsection{Saturated classes}\label{section5.5}
\noindent To verify the homotopy lifting-extension axiom, we adapt the proof of the homotopy lifting-extension axiom for simplicial sets in \cite[Chapter I, Proposition 5.2]{GoerssJardine1999}. The proof for simplicial sets uses saturated classes of monomorphisms of simplicial sets, which are defined as follows.

\begin{definition}[{\cite[Chapter IV, Definition 2.1]{GZ1967}}]\label{defsatclassdef} A class $S$ of monomorphisms of simplicial sets is \emph{saturated} if the following conditions are met.

\begin{enumerate}

\item All isomorphisms are contained in $S$.

\item In a pushout square
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	A \&\& {A'} \\
	\\
	B \&\& {{B'},}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["m"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{m'}"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent if $m$ is contained in $S$, then $m'$ is contained in $S$.

\item In a section-retraction commutative diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{A'} \&\& A \&\& {A'} \\
	\\
	{B'} \&\& B \&\& {{B'},}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{m'}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-5]
	\arrow["m"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["{m'}"', from=1-5, to=3-5]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-3, to=3-5]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent if $m$ is contained in $S$, then $m'$ is contained in $S$.

\item Given a sequence of composable maps $$A_{1} \xrightarrow{m_{1}} A_{2} \xrightarrow{m_{2}} \cdots,$$ if $m_{t}$ is contained in $S$ for every positive integer $t$, then so is $A_{1} \rightarrow \operatorname{colim} (A_{n})$.

\item Given an indexing set $L$ and $m_{l} \colon A_{l} \rightarrow B_{l}$ for $l \in L$, if $m_{l}$ is contained in $S$ for every $l \in L$, then $$\coprod\limits_{l \in L} m_{l} \colon \coprod\limits_{l \in L} A_{l} \rightarrow \coprod\limits_{l \in L} B_{l}$$ is contained in $S$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{definition}
\noindent As the intersection of saturated classes forms another saturated class, the following definition is well-posed.

\begin{definition}\label{defsaturationdef} The \emph{saturation} of a class $P$ of monomorphisms of simplicial sets is $$\langle P \rangle=\bigcap\limits_{\substack{S \supset P \textrm{ and} \\
S \textrm{ is saturated }}} S.$$
\end{definition}
\noindent Saturated classes provide us with descriptions of the class of acyclic cofibrations of simplicial sets.

\begin{proposition}[{\cite[Chapter I, Proposition 4.2]{GoerssJardine1999}}]\label{satclasprop} Consider the monomorphism classes of simplicial sets

\begin{enumerate}

\item $P_{1}=\left\{j_{n, k} \colon \Lambda[n, k] \rightarrow \Delta[n] \mid n \geq 1 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\}$; and

\item $P_{2}=\left\{\left(\Delta[1] \times \partial\Delta[n]\right) \cup \left(\{e\} \times \Delta[n]\right) \rightarrow \Delta[1] \times \Delta[n] \mid e \in \{0, 1\} \textrm{ and } n \geq 0\right\}$.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Then, $\langle P_{1} \rangle=\langle P_{2} \rangle$ is the class of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$.
\end{proposition}
\noindent Attaching a disjoint basepoint yields a description of the class of acyclic cofibrations of pointed simplicial sets.

\begin{corollary}\label{satclaspropcor} Consider the monomorphism classes of pointed simplicial sets

\begin{enumerate}

\item $(P_{1})_{+}=\left\{(j_{n, k})_{+} \colon \Lambda[n, k]_{+} \rightarrow \Delta[n]_{+} \mid n \geq 1 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\}$; and

\item $(P_{2})_{+}=\left\{\left(\left(\Delta[1] \times \partial\Delta[n]\right) \cup \left(\{e\} \times \Delta[n]\right)\right)_{+} \rightarrow \left(\Delta[1] \times \Delta[n]\right)_{+} \mid e \in \{0, 1\} \textrm{ and } n \geq 0\right\}$.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Then, $\langle (P_{1})_{+} \rangle=\langle (P_{2})_{+} \rangle$ is the class of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$.
\end{corollary}
\noindent We use Corollary \ref{satclaspropcor} to prove a lemma that underpins our verification of the homotopy lifting-extension axiom.

\begin{lemma}\label{appworthy} For two pointed simplicial maps $i \colon A \rightarrow B$ and $j \colon X \rightarrow Y$, if both of them are monomorphisms, then the induced map
$$\left(A \wedge Y\right) \underset{\left(A \wedge X\right)}{\bigcup} \left(B \wedge X\right) \rightarrow B \wedge Y$$
is a monomorphism, which is also a weak homotopy equivalence if $i$ or $j$ is also a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} If both $i$ and $j$ are monomorphisms, then so is the induced map. To prove the second part of the claim, the key idea is to write $P$ for the class of monomorphisms $A' \rightarrow B'$ of pointed simplicial sets such that $$\left(A' \wedge Y\right) \underset{\left(A \wedge X\right)}{\bigcup} \left(B' \wedge X\right) \rightarrow B' \wedge Y$$ is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$. We show that $P$ contains the class of acyclic cofibrations in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$. As $P$ is a saturated class, thus equal to its own saturation, Corollary \ref{satclaspropcor} truncates our task to showing that $$(P_{2})_{+} \subset P.$$ Thus, for $e \in \{0, 1\}$ and $n \geq 0$, we need to show that
$$\left(\left(\left(\Delta[1] \times \partial\Delta[n]\right) \cup \left(\{e\} \times \Delta[n]\right)\right)_{+} \wedge Y\right) \cup \left(\left(\Delta[1] \times \Delta[n]\right)_{+} \wedge X\right) \rightarrow \left(\Delta[1] \times \Delta[n]\right)_{+} \wedge Y$$ is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$. We use Lemma \ref{smashanddisjoint} to write down the commutative diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\left(\left(\left(\Delta[1] \times \partial\Delta[n]\right) \cup \left(\{e\} \times \Delta[n]\right)\right)_{+} \wedge Y\right) \cup \left(\left(\Delta[1] \times \Delta[n]\right)_{+} \wedge X\right)} \&\& {\left(\Delta[1] \times \Delta[n]\right)_{+} \wedge Y} \\
	\\
	{\left(\left(\left(\Delta[1]_{+} \wedge \partial\Delta[n]_{+}\right) \cup \left(\{e\}_{+} \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}\right)\right) \wedge Y\right) \cup \left(\left(\Delta[1]_{+} \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}\right) \wedge X\right)} \&\& {\left(\Delta[1]_{+} \wedge \Delta[n]_{+}\right) \wedge Y} \\
	\\
	{\left(\Delta[1]_{+} \wedge \left(\left(\partial\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge Y\right) \cup \left(\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge X\right)\right)\right) \cup \left(\{e\}_{+} \wedge \left(\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge Y\right) \right)} \&\& {\Delta[1]_{+} \wedge \left(\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge Y\right),}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["\cong"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["\cong"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["\cong"', from=3-1, to=5-1]
	\arrow["\cong"', from=3-3, to=5-3]
	\arrow[from=5-1, to=5-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where the upper pair of vertical isomorphisms is an instance of Lemma \ref{smashanddisjoint} and $$\left(\Delta[1]_{+} \wedge \left(\left(\partial\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge Y\right) \cup \left(\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge X\right)\right)\right) \cup \left(\{e\}_{+} \wedge \left(\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge Y\right) \right) \rightarrow \Delta[1]_{+} \wedge \left(\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge Y\right)$$
is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ because its geometric realization is the inclusion of a strong deformation retract in pointed topological spaces. Overall, we conclude that $(P_{2})_{+} \subset P$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Verification of the homotopy lifting-extension axiom}\label{section5.6}
\noindent We are now ready to verify that the structure defined in Section \ref{section5.2} also satisfies the homotopy lifting-extension axiom.

\begin{proposition}\label{simplicialaxiomm7} If $i \colon A \rightarrow B$ is a cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ and $p \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, then the induced map
$$\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(B, X\right) \xrightarrow{(i^{\ast}, p_{\ast})} \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(A, X\right) \underset{\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(A, Y\right)}{\times} \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(B, Y\right)$$
is a Kan fibration, which is also a weak homotopy equivalence if $i$ or $p$ is also a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} To prove that the induced map is a Kan fibration, we solve a lifting problem of the form
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=6mm, column sep=6mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Lambda[n, k]} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(B, X\right)} \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n]} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(A, X\right) \underset{\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(A, Y\right)}{\times} \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(B, Y\right).}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{j_{n, k}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{(i^{\ast}, p_{\ast})}"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent The adjunction $(-)_{+} \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \colon V$ from Lemma \ref{pointtheunpointed} yields the equivalent lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=6mm, column sep=6mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\Lambda[n, k]_{+}} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(B, X\right)} \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n]_{+}} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(A, X\right) \underset{\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(A, Y\right)}{\times} \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(B, Y\right).}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{\left(j_{n, k}\right)_{+}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{(i^{\ast}, p_{\ast})}"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent We apply Corollary \ref{pointedexplawcor} to infer that this new lifting problem is, in turn, equivalent to the lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\left(\Lambda[n, k]_{+} \wedge B\right) \underset{\left(\Lambda[n, k]_{+} \wedge A\right)}{\bigcup} \left(\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge A\right)} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge B} \&\& {Y.}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["p"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent By Lemma \ref{appworthy}, the left-hand map is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ as $\left(j_{n, k}\right)_{+}$ is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$. As $p$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, our lifting problem can be solved, so the induced map is a Kan fibration. \\ \\
If $i$ or $p$ is also a weak homotopy equivalence, then we prove that the induced map is also a weak homotopy equivalence. To that end, we solve a lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=6mm, column sep=6mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\partial\Delta[n]} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(B, X\right)} \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n]} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(A, X\right) \underset{\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(A, Y\right)}{\times} \operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}\left(B, Y\right).}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{i_{n}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{(i^{\ast}, p_{\ast})}"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent Another consecutive application of the adjunctions from Lemma \ref{pointtheunpointed} and Corollary \ref{pointedexplawcor} translates our lifting problem to the equivalent lifting problem
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\left(\partial\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge B\right) \underset{\left(\partial\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge A\right)}{\bigcup} \left(\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge A\right)} \&\& X \\
	\\
	{\Delta[n]_{+} \wedge B} \&\& {Y.}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["p"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[dashed, from=3-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent By Lemma \ref{appworthy}, the left-hand map is a cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ because $\left(i_{n}\right)_{+}$ is a cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$. Depending on whether $i$ or $p$ is also a weak homotopy equivalence, we have two cases to scrutinize.
\begin{enumerate}

\item If $i$ is also a weak homotopy equivalence, then, by Lemma \ref{appworthy}, the left-hand map is an acyclic cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. As $p$ is a fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, our lifting problem can be solved.

\item If $p$ is also a weak homotopy equivalence, then $p$ is an acyclic fibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. As the left-hand map is a cofibration in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$, our lifting problem can be solved.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent In either case, the induced map is both a Kan fibration and a weak homotopy equivalence, as required.
\end{proof}
\noindent By Propositions \ref{simplicialaxiomm6} and \ref{simplicialaxiomm7}, our definitions in Section \ref{section5.2} endow ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ with a simplicial model structure.

\begin{theorem}\label{simplicialdefsworks} Let ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ denote the model category from Theorem \ref{rightinduceeyebrow}. We define

\begin{enumerate}

\item the function complex of two $1$-reduced simplicial sets $X$ and $Y$ to be $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(X, Y)$;

\item the tensor of a $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ with a simplicial set $Z$ to be $X \wedge Z_{+}$; and

\item the cotensor of a $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ with a simplicial set $Z$ to be $E_{1}\left(\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}}(Z_{+}, X)\right)$.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent With these definitions, ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ has the structure of a simplicial model category.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof} With our definition of function complexes, ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is enriched over simplicial sets as ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ is enriched over simplicial sets. By Propositions \ref{simplicialaxiomm6} and \ref{simplicialaxiomm7}, our definitions satisfy the simplicial model category axioms.
\end{proof}

\section{Rational homotopy theory of $1$-connected spaces}\label{section6}
\noindent From now on, let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. We gather the background and auxiliary results from the rational homotopy theory of $1$-connected topological spaces that we need to recover the entirety of Quillen's family of model structures on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. In particular, we characterize $M$-local spaces and $M$-local homotopy equivalences in terms of function complexes, which prepares us for the left Bousfield localization in Section \ref{section7}. Even though we do not expect these characterizations to be novel to the rational homotopy theory expert, we were unable to find them in the literature, so we provide a full proof.

\subsection{Localization of $1$-connected spaces}\label{section6.1}
\noindent We first review some concepts from \cite[Chapter 9]{FHT2001}.

\begin{definition}\label{defrhtdef} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$.

\begin{enumerate}

\item A map of $1$-connected spaces $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is an \emph{$M$-local homotopy equivalence} if, for every $n \geq 2$, the induced map $$f_{\ast} \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} \operatorname{id}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \colon \pi_{n}(X) \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} M^{-1}\mathbb{Z} \rightarrow \pi_{n}(Y) \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$$ is an isomorphism.

\item A $1$-connected space $Y$ is \emph{$M$-local} if, for every $n \geq 2$, the abelian group $\pi_{n}(Y)$ is an $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$-module.

\item An \emph{$M$-localization} of a $1$-connected space $Y$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence $f \colon Y \rightarrow Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ with $M$-local target.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent When $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$, we speak of a \emph{rational homotopy equivalence}, a \emph{rational space}, and a \emph{rationalization}.

\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{defrhtdef1} If $f \colon Y \rightarrow Y_{\mathbb{Q}}$ is a rationalization of $Y$, then, for every $n \geq 2$, $f$ induces the isomorphism $$f_{\ast} \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} \operatorname{id}_{\mathbb{Q}} \colon \pi_{n}(Y) \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} \mathbb{Q} \xrightarrow{\cong} \pi_{n}(Y_{\mathbb{Q}})$$
that discards all torsion in the $n$-th homotopy group of $Y$ in a purely topological fashion.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}\label{defrhtdef2} Every weak homotopy equivalence of $1$-connected spaces is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence. Also, every $M$-local homotopy equivalence of $M$-local spaces is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{example}

\begin{example}\label{defrhtdef3} For $n \geq 2$, the \emph{$M$-local $n$-sphere} $S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is an $M$-local CW complex containing the $n$-sphere $S^{n}$ as a CW subcomplex, and the inclusion $S^{n} \rightarrow S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is an $M$-localization of $S^{n}$.
\end{example}
\noindent Every $1$-connected space can be $M$-localized uniquely up to homotopy equivalence as follows.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 9.7]{FHT2001}}]\label{thebigdeal} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Let $Y$ be a $1$-connected space.

\begin{enumerate}

\item There exists a relative CW complex $\left(Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Y\right)$ with no $0$-cells and no $1$-cells such that the inclusion $Y \rightarrow Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is an $M$-localization of $Y$.

\item Every map $f \colon Y \rightarrow Z$ with $M$-local target extends to a map $g \colon Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \rightarrow Z$ such that the diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	Y \&\& {Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}} \\
	\\
	\&\& {Z}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["g"', dashed, from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent commutes, and every homotopy $f \simeq f'$ extends to a homotopy $g \simeq g'$. Thus, every map $f \colon Y \rightarrow Z$ with $M$-local target extends to a map $g \colon Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \rightarrow Z$ uniquely up to homotopy.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent In particular, any other space $Y'_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ satisfying (1) and (2) is homotopy equivalent (relative to $Y$) to $Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\label{cwbusiness} Note that, if $Y$ is a $1$-connected CW complex, then so is $Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{A characterization of local spaces}\label{section6.2}
\noindent For a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, we characterize $M$-local spaces in a way that supports our model-categorical arguments for the left Bousfield localization in Section \ref{section7}. \\ \\
For two topological spaces $X$ and $Y$, the {function complex} $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X, Y\right)$$ is the simplicial set with $n$-simplices $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X, Y\right)_{n}=\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X \times |\Delta[n]|, Y\right).$$ 
In particular, observe that $$\pi_{0}\left(\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X, Y\right)\right)=\left[X, Y\right]$$
is the set of homotopy classes of maps from $X$ to $Y$. \\ \\
The following lemma is a straightforward application of the universal property of $M$-localization.

\begin{lemma}\label{ineedthisonetoo} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Let $X$ and $Y$ be $1$-connected spaces. If $Y$ is $M$-local, then the map $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Y\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X, Y\right)$$ induced by the inclusion $X \rightarrow X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{lemma}
\noindent We are now ready to establish a characterization of $M$-local spaces in the language of function complexes.

\begin{proposition}\label{illdothisone} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. A $1$-connected space $Y$ is $M$-local if and only if, for every $n \geq 2$, the map $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Y\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}, Y\right)$$ induced by the inclusion $S^{n} \rightarrow S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} If $Y$ is $M$-local, then Lemma \ref{ineedthisonetoo} implies that, for $n \geq 2$, the map $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Y\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}, Y\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence. Conversely, suppose that, for $n \geq 2$, the map $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Y\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}, Y\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence. Let $\tau \colon S^{n} \rightarrow S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ denote the inclusion. Then, for every map $f \colon S^{n} \rightarrow Y$, there exists a map $g \colon S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \rightarrow Y$, unique up to homotopy, such that the diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{S^{n}} \&\& {S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}} \\
	\\
	\&\& Y
	\arrow["{\tau}", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["g"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent commutes up to homotopy. For $n \geq 2$, let $[f]$ be an element of $\pi_{n}(Y)$ represented by a map $f \colon S^{n} \rightarrow Y$, and let $m \in M$. Then, there exists a map $g \colon S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \rightarrow Y$, unique up to homotopy, such that the diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{S^{n}} \&\& {S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}} \\
	\\
	\&\& Y
	\arrow["{\tau}", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["g"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent commutes up to homotopy. In particular, homotopy commutativity implies that $$[f]=f_{\ast}\left([\operatorname{id}_{S^{n}}]\right)=g_{\ast}\tau_{\ast}\left([\operatorname{id}_{S^{n}}]\right)=g_{\ast}\left([\tau]\right) \in \pi_{n}(Y).$$
As $S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is $M$-local, there exists a unique element $$\dfrac{1}{m} \cdot [\tau] \in \pi_{n}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}) \cong \pi_{n}(S^{n}) \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} M^{-1}\mathbb{Z} \cong \mathbb{Z} \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} M^{-1}\mathbb{Z} \cong M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}.$$
Therefore, we uniquely define
$$\dfrac{1}{m} \cdot [f]=g_{\ast}\left(\dfrac{1}{m} \cdot [\tau]\right) \in \pi_{n}(Y).$$
We conclude that the $1$-connected space $Y$ is $M$-local.
\end{proof}
\noindent As our function complexes of $1$-reduced simplicial sets are pointed, we need the pointed version of Proposition \ref{illdothisone}. We write $\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}$ for the category of pointed spaces. For two pointed spaces $X$ and $Y$, the function complex $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(X, Y\right)$$ is the simplicial set with $n$-simplices $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(X, Y\right)_{n}=\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(X \wedge |\Delta[n]|_{+}, Y\right).$$
Observe that $$\pi_{0}(\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(X, Y\right))=\left[X, Y\right]_{\ast}$$
is the set of pointed homotopy classes of pointed maps from $X$ to $Y$. \\ \\
In terms of these function complexes, we state the pointed version of Proposition \ref{illdothisone}, which is proven identically.

\begin{proposition}\label{illdothisonept} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. A $1$-connected pointed space $Y$ is $M$-local if and only if, for every $n \geq 2$, the map $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Y\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(S^{n}, Y\right)$$ induced by the inclusion $S^{n} \rightarrow S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{A characterization of local homotopy equivalences}\label{section6.3}
\noindent For a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, we also characterize the $M$-local homotopy equivalences in preparation for the left Bousfield localization in Section \ref{section7}. \\ \\
The following lemma, in conjunction with its pointed analog, underpins our arguments.

\begin{lemma}[{\cite[Corollary 9.3.3(2)]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{citeadnauseam}

\begin{enumerate}

\item If $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is a weak homotopy equivalence of simplicial sets and $Z$ is a Kan complex, then $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}(Y, Z) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}(X, Z)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item If $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is a weak homotopy equivalence of CW complexes and $Z$ is a topological space, then $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(Y, Z) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(X, Z)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\end{enumerate}

\end{lemma}
\noindent We provide a series of characterizations of $M$-local homotopy equivalences in terms of function complexes.

\begin{proposition}\label{illdothisonetoo} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a map of $1$-connected CW complexes. The following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The map $f$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence.

\item The induced map on $M$-localizations $$f_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \colon X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \rightarrow Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$$
is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item The induced map on $M$-localizations $$f_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \colon X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \rightarrow Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$$
is a homotopy equivalence.

\item For every $M$-local space $Z$, the map $$f_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Z\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Z\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item For every $M$-local space $Z$, the map $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(Y, Z\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X, Z\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\end{enumerate}

\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Firstly, (1) and (2) are equivalent by applying Example \ref{defrhtdef2} to the commutative diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=6mm, column sep=6mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	X \&\& Y \\
	\\
	{X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}} \&\& {Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}}
	\arrow["f", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["{f_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}}", from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent whose vertical maps are $M$-local homotopy equivalences. Then, (2) and (3) are equivalent by the Whitehead theorem, as both $X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ and $Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ are CW complexes by Remark \ref{cwbusiness}. Moreover, (4) and (5) are equivalent because, for an $M$-local space $Z$, we have the commutative diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=6mm, column sep=6mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Z\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Z\right)} \\
	\\
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(Y, Z\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X, Z\right)}
	\arrow["{f_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}^{\ast}}", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["{f^{\ast}}", from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent whose vertical maps are weak homotopy equivalences by Lemma \ref{ineedthisonetoo}. \\ \\
It remains to show that (3) and (4) are equivalent. If (3) holds, then Lemma \ref{citeadnauseam}(2) informs us that, for every ($M$-local) space $Z$, the map $$f_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Z\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Z\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence. Conversely, if (4) holds, then using the induced bijection of path components
$$f_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}^{\ast} \colon \left[Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Z\right] \xrightarrow{\cong} \left[X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Z\right]$$
for $Z=X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ and $Z=Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ yields a homotopy inverse of $f_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$.
\end{proof}
\noindent It is the equivalence of (1) and (5) in Proposition \ref{illdothisonetoo} that is relevant to our work in Section \ref{section7}. Again, we need the pointed version of that equivalence, which is proven identically.

\begin{proposition}\label{illdothisonetoopt} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. A pointed map of $1$-connected CW complexes $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence if and only if, for every $M$-local pointed space $Z$, the map $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(Y, Z\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(X, Z\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{proposition}

\section{The family of model structures on $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section7}
\noindent Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. We left Bousfield localize the model category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ to get a model category $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ whose weak equivalences are the $M$-local homotopy equivalences. In particular, we obtain a model category $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ whose weak equivalences are the rational homotopy equivalences. Thus, we recover the family of model structures on $1$-reduced simplicial sets from \cite[Part II, Theorem 2.2]{Quillen1969}. These model structures inherit the generating cofibrations and the simplicial structure that we found for ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.

\subsection{Left Bousfield localization}\label{section7.1} We give an overview of left Bousfield localization; a reference is \cite[Chapters 1-4]{Hirschhorn2003}. Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a simplicial model category and $T$ be a set of morphisms in $\mathcal{M}$.

\begin{definition}\label{deflblsdef}

\begin{enumerate}

\item An object $K$ of $\mathcal{M}$ is \emph{$T$-local} if $K$ is fibrant in $\mathcal{M}$ and, for every morphism $\varphi \colon A \rightarrow B$ in $T$, the map $$\varphi^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(B, K\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(A, K\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item A morphism $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ in $\mathcal{M}$ is a \emph{$T$-local equivalence} if, for every $T$-local object $K$, the map $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(Y, K\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{M}}\left(X, K\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item A \emph{$T$-localization} of an object $X$ of $\mathcal{M}$ is a $T$-local equivalence $f \colon X \rightarrow X_{T}$ with $T$-local target.

\end{enumerate}

\end{definition}

\begin{example}\label{deflblsdef1} Every morphism in $T$ and every weak equivalence in $\mathcal{M}$ is a $T$-local equivalence.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 4.7]{Barwick2010}}]\label{lblexists} Let $\mathcal{M}$ be a combinatorial, left proper, and simplicial model category. Let $T$ be a set of morphisms in $\mathcal{M}$. There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{T}\mathcal{M}$ on the underlying category of $\mathcal{M}$, called the \emph{left Bousfield localization of $\mathcal{M}$ at $T$}, in which a morphism $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is a $T$-local equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a cofibration in $\mathcal{M}$.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent An object $K$ is fibrant in $\mathcal{L}_{T}\mathcal{M}$ if and only if $K$ is $T$-local. The model category $\mathcal{L}_{T}\mathcal{M}$ is combinatorial and left proper, and it inherits the simplicial structure of $\mathcal{M}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\label{lblexistsrmk} Note that a $T$-localization in $\mathcal{M}$ is a fibrant replacement in $\mathcal{L}_{T}\mathcal{M}$.
\end{remark}
\noindent The left Bousfield localization of $\mathcal{M}$ at $T$ enlarges, in a systematic fashion, the class of weak equivalences of $\mathcal{M}$ to encompass the morphisms in the set $T$. Observe that $\mathcal{L}_{T}\mathcal{M}$ inherits the generating cofibrations of $\mathcal{M}$, but it generally does not also inherit the generating acyclic cofibrations of $\mathcal{M}$ as, in general, the class of acyclic cofibrations in $\mathcal{M}$ is properly contained in the class of acyclic cofibrations in $\mathcal{L}_{T}\mathcal{M}$.

\subsection{The set for the left Bousfield localization}\label{section7.2} For a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, our characterization of $M$-local spaces from Proposition \ref{illdothisonept} suggests that we produce a $1$-reduced simplicial analog of the set of maps $$\left\{S^{n} \rightarrow S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \mid n \geq 2\right\}$$ in order to left Bousfield localize the model category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ at that set. \\ \\
A sensible first suggestion is to start with the simplicial $n$-sphere $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$ and use it to emulate the construction of $S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ in the $1$-reduced setting. This approach runs into a problem: the construction of $S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ involves self-maps of $S^{n}$ of arbitrary degree, whereas the simplicial $n$-sphere $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$ only has the trivial self-maps.

\begin{lemma}\label{oopsnoselfmaps} For $n \geq 2$, the only self-maps of $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$ are the constant map and the identity map.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} Let $f \colon S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n} \rightarrow S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$ be a self-map of $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$. Let $x$ denote the unique $0$-simplex of $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$ and let $\epsilon$ denote the non-degenerate $n$-simplex of $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$. There are two possibilities for the $n$-simplex $f_{n}(\epsilon)$ of $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$.

\begin{enumerate}

\item If $f_{n}(\epsilon)=\epsilon$, then $f$ is the identity map of $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$.

\item If $f_{n}(\epsilon)$ is the iterated degeneracy of $x$, then $f$ is the constant self-map of $S_{\mathrm{simp}}^{n}$.

\end{enumerate}

\end{proof}
\noindent Thus, we have to settle for an indirect route. We start with the CW inclusion $$S^{n} \rightarrow S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}.$$
We apply the singular complex functor to obtain the inclusion of simplicial sets $$\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}).$$
Then, we apply the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex functor to obtain the inclusion of $1$-reduced simplicial sets $$E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}) \rightarrow E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}).$$
To confirm that this map is the analog we are looking for, we study its geometric realization
$$|E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})| \rightarrow |E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|.$$
In particular, we study the commutative diagram of CW complexes
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{|E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|} \&\& {|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|} \&\& {S^{n}} \\
	\\
	{|E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|} \&\& {|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|} \&\& {S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}},}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-5]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=1-5, to=3-5]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-3, to=3-5]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where the horizontal maps in the left-hand square are CW subcomplex inclusions and the horizontal maps in the right-hand square are natural maps from the geometric realization-singular complex adjunction. We show that all the horizontal maps are weak homotopy equivalences.

\begin{proposition}\label{comdiagramheqs} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Let $n \geq 2$. In the commutative diagram
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{|E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|} \&\& {|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|} \&\& {S^{n}} \\
	\\
	{|E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|} \&\& {|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|} \&\& {S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}},}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-5]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=1-5, to=3-5]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-3, to=3-5]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent all the horizontal maps are weak homotopy equivalences.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} By Corollary \ref{mo67cor}, the horizontal maps in the left-hand square are weak homotopy equivalences. By Theorem \ref{quilleneqsecretly}(2), the horizontal maps in the right-hand square are weak homotopy equivalences, as well.
\end{proof}
\noindent Proposition \ref{comdiagramheqs} translates to the success of our approach. Thus, for a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, we left Bousfield localize ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ at the set of maps $$T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}=\left\{E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}) \rightarrow E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}) \mid n \geq 2\right\}.$$

\subsection{The family of model structures on $1$-reduced simplicial sets}\label{section7.3} For a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, we describe the left Bousfield localization of the model category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ at the set of maps $$T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}=\left\{E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}) \rightarrow E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}) \mid n \geq 2\right\}.$$ In particular, we describe the $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local objects and the $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local equivalences in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$. To state our characterizations, we translate the definitions from Section \ref{section6.1} from the language of $1$-connected topological spaces to that of $1$-reduced simplicial sets using geometric realization.

\begin{definition}\label{defrhtssetsdef} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$.

\begin{enumerate}

\item A map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets $f$ is an \emph{$M$-local homotopy equivalence} if $|f|$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence of $1$-connected spaces.

\item A $1$-reduced simplicial set $K$ is \emph{$M$-local} if $K$ is a Kan complex and $|K|$ is an $M$-local space.

\item An \emph{$M$-localization} of a $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence $f \colon X \rightarrow X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ with $M$-local target.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent When $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$, we speak of a \emph{rational homotopy equivalence}, a \emph{rational simplicial set}, and a \emph{rationalization}.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}\label{defrhtssetsdef3} By Proposition \ref{comdiagramheqs}, every map in $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is an $M$-localization. Note that Corollary \ref{forlaterusetbacor} says that the target of every map in $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is a Kan complex.
\end{example}
\noindent The following proposition helps us transfer the rational homotopy theory of $1$-connected spaces from Section \ref{section6} to $1$-reduced simplicial sets.

\begin{proposition}[{\cite[Proposition 1.1.11]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{pshsaveme} If $A$ and $X$ are pointed simplicial sets and $X$ is a Kan complex, then there is a weak homotopy equivalence $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(A, X\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|A|, |X|\right)$$
that is natural in $A$ and in $X$.
\end{proposition}
\noindent By a series of characterizations, we show that a $1$-reduced simplicial set is $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local if and only if it is $M$-local.

\begin{proposition}\label{charthelocals} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Let $X$ be a $1$-reduced simplicial set. The following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local.

\item The $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex such that, for every $n \geq 2$, the map

$$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}), X\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}), X\right)$$
is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item The $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex such that, for every $n \geq 2$, the map

$$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}), X\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}), X\right)$$
is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item The $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex such that, for every $n \geq 2$, the map

$$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|, |X|\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|, |X|\right)$$
is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item The $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex such that, for every $n \geq 2$, the map

$$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, |X|) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(S^{n}, |X|)$$
is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item The $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex such that $|X|$ is an $M$-local space.

\item The $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is $M$-local.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Hence, $X$ is $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local if and only if $X$ is $M$-local.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Firstly, (1) and (2) are equivalent by the definition of a $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local object in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ combined with the characterization of fibrant objects in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ from Theorem \ref{rightinduceeyebrow}. Then, (2) and (3) are equivalent by the commutative diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}), X\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}), X\right)} \\
	\\
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}), X\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}), X\right),}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent whose horizontal maps are weak homotopy equivalences by Proposition \ref{comdiagramheqs}. Moreover, (3) and (4) are equivalent by the commutative diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}), X\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|, |X|\right)} \\
	\\
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}), X\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|, |X|\right),}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent whose horizontal maps are weak homotopy equivalences by Proposition \ref{pshsaveme}. Furthermore, (4) and (5) are equivalent by the commutative diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, |X|)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|, |X|\right)} \\
	\\
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(S^{n}, |X|)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|, |X|\right),}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent whose horizontal maps are weak homotopy equivalences by Proposition \ref{comdiagramheqs}. Also, (5) and (6) are equivalent by Proposition \ref{illdothisonept}. Lastly, (6) and (7) are equivalent by the definition of an $M$-local simplicial set.
\end{proof}
\noindent By a similar series of characterizations, we establish that a map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets is a $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local equivalence if and only if it is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence. To that end, we need two auxiliary lemmas.

\begin{lemma}\label{usefulcorfyinfo} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. If $Z$ is an $M$-local pointed space, then $E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Z)$ is an $M$-local simplicial set.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} By Corollary \ref{forlaterusetbacor}, $E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Z)$ is a Kan complex. Then, Corollary \ref{mo67cor} and Theorem \ref{quilleneqsecretly}(2) provide the weak homotopy equivalences $$|E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Z)| \rightarrow |\operatorname{Sing}(Z)| \rightarrow Z,$$
so $|E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Z)|$ is $M$-local. Overall, $E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Z)$ is $M$-local, as required.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{omgwhatshappening} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. The following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item For every $M$-local simplicial set $K$, the map $$|f|^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|Y|, |K|) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|X|, |K|)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item For every $M$-local pointed space $Z$, the map $$|f|^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|Y|, Z) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|X|, Z)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\end{enumerate}

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} Suppose (1) holds. Let $Z$ be an $M$-local pointed space. Then, by Lemma \ref{usefulcorfyinfo}, $E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Z)$ is an $M$-local simplicial set. There is a commutative diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|Y|, |E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Z)|\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|Y|, |\operatorname{Sing}(Z)|\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|Y|, Z)} \\
	\\
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|X|, |E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(Z)|\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|X|, |\operatorname{Sing}(Z)|\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|X|, Z)}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=1-5]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=1-5, to=3-5]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-3, to=3-5]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent whose horizontal maps are weak homotopy equivalences by Corollary \ref{mo67cor} and Theorem \ref{quilleneqsecretly}(2) and whose left-hand vertical map is a weak homotopy equivalence by (1). Thus, the right-hand vertical map is a weak homotopy equivalence, which proves (2). \\ \\
Conversely, suppose (2) holds. Let $K$ be an $M$-local simplicial set. Then, $|K|$ is an $M$-local pointed space, so $$|f|^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|Y|, |K|\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}\left(|X|, |K|\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence, which proves (1).
\end{proof}
\noindent We are now ready for the series of characterizations that describes the $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local equivalences.

\begin{proposition}\label{chartheloceqs} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. The following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The map $f$ is a $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local equivalence.

\item For every $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local simplicial set $K$, the map $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}(Y, K) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}(X, K)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item For every $M$-local simplicial set $K$, the map $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}(Y, K) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}(X, K)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item For every $M$-local simplicial set $K$, the map $$|f|^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|Y|, |K|) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|X|, |K|)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item For every $M$-local pointed space $Z$, the map $$|f|^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|Y|, Z) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|X|, Z)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item The map $|f|$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence.

\item The map $f$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Hence, $f$ is a $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local equivalence if and only $f$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Firstly, (1) and (2) are equivalent by the definition of a $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local equivalence. Then, (2) and (3) are equivalent by Proposition \ref{charthelocals}. Now, (3) and (4) are equivalent by the commutative diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}(Y, K)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|Y|, |K|)} \\
	\\
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}}(X, K)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}_{\ast}}(|X|, |K|),}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{f^{\ast}}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{|f|^{\ast}}"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent whose horizontal maps are weak homotopy equivalences by Proposition \ref{pshsaveme}. Moreover, (4) and (5) are equivalent by Lemma \ref{omgwhatshappening}. Furthermore, (5) and (6) are equivalent by Proposition \ref{illdothisonetoopt}. Lastly, (6) and (7) are equivalent by the definition of an $M$-local homotopy equivalence.
\end{proof}
\noindent Propositions \ref{charthelocals} and \ref{chartheloceqs} together describe the left Bousfield localization of ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ at the set $T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$.

\begin{theorem}\label{ididitididit} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ on the category of $1$-reduced simplicial sets in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent A $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is fibrant in $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ if and only if $X$ is $M$-local. The model category $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is combinatorial, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Theorem \ref{simplicialdefsworks}.
\end{theorem}
\noindent Theorem \ref{ididitididit} recovers the family of model structures on $1$-reduced simplicial sets in \cite[Part II, Theorem 2.2]{Quillen1969}, as well as the characterization of fibrant objects in \cite[Part II, Corollary 2.6]{Quillen1969}. The most prominent model structure in this family is that corresponding to the case $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$, so we present it separately.

\begin{theorem}\label{ididitididitq} There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ on the category of $1$-reduced simplicial sets in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is a rational homotopy equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent A $1$-reduced simplicial set $X$ is fibrant in $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ if and only if $X$ is rational. The model category $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is combinatorial, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Theorem \ref{simplicialdefsworks}.
\end{theorem}

\section{An approach using localization with respect to homology}\label{section8}
\noindent We give an alternative description of the family of model structures from Theorem \ref{ididitididit}. This description uses Bousfield localization with respect to homology, as developed in \cite{Bousfield1975}, together with Theorem \ref{leftinducetheorem} on left-induced model structures. The idea is that, instead of first left-transferring the model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ and then localizing, we can first localize the model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ and then left-transfer it. As a pointed simplicial set need not be $1$-connected, we do not localize ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast}$ with respect to homotopy, but rather with respect to homology. Because the two notions are in agreement for $1$-connected spaces, the left transfer to ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ yields the model structures from Theorem \ref{ididitididit}, and we extract our alternative description.

\subsection{Bousfield localization with respect to homology}\label{section8.1} Firstly, we introduce the following terminology.

\begin{definition}\label{defhlgyisodef} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$.

\begin{enumerate}

\item A map of topological spaces $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is an \emph{$M$-local homology isomorphism} if, for every $n \geq 0$, the induced map $$f_{\ast} \colon H_{n}(X; M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}) \rightarrow H_{n}(Y; M^{-1}\mathbb{Z})$$ is an isomorphism.

\item A simplicial map $g$ is an \emph{$M$-local homology isomorphism} if $|g|$ is an $M$-local homology isomorphism of topological spaces.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent When $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$, we speak of a \emph{rational homology isomorphism}.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{defhlgyisodef1} As the abelian group $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$ is flat \cite[Theorem 4.80]{Rotman2009}, the universal coefficient theorem for homology yields the natural isomorphism $$H_{n}(X; M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}) \cong H_{n}(X; \mathbb{Z}) \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}.$$
Thus, $M$-local homology discards torsion from integral homology in the same fashion $M$-local homotopy discards torsion from higher homotopy groups. When $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$, all torsion is discarded in both cases.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}\label{defhlgyisodef2} By Remark \ref{defhlgyisodef1}, the unique map from $\mathbb{R}P^{2}$ to the point is a rational homology isomorphism.
\end{example}
\noindent The natural isomorphism from Remark \ref{defhlgyisodef1} also has the following consequence that we note for later use.

\begin{lemma}\label{forlateruselemma} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Every integral homology isomorphism is an $M$-local homology isomorphism.
\end{lemma}
\noindent Also for later use, we need to recall the following theorem that relates integral homology and homotopy.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Corollary 6.69(1)]{DavisKirk2001}}]\label{dkcited} Every weak homotopy equivalence is an integral homology isomorphism.
\end{theorem}
\noindent We also need to recall another theorem of a similar nature for future reference. This theorem informs us that, under the hypothesis of $1$-connectedness, $M$-local homology agrees with $M$-local homotopy.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 8.6]{FHT2001}}]\label{whiteheadserrepair} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. A map of $1$-connected spaces $f$ is an $M$-local homology isomorphism if and only if $f$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence.
\end{theorem}
\noindent For a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, Bousfield localization with respect to homology endows the category $\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ of simplicial sets with a model structure whose weak equivalences are the $M$-local homology isomorphisms.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 10.2]{Bousfield1975}}]\label{unptedhomloc} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ on the category of simplicial sets in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is an $M$-local homology isomorphism; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent This model category is combinatorial, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Example \ref{defsmcdef1}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\label{unptedhomlocrmk} By \cite[\S 2.5]{Bousfield1997}, $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ is the left Bousfield localization of $\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ at a single map, which allows us to infer that $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ inherits the properties of being combinatorial, left proper, and simplicial.
\end{remark}
\noindent When $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$, we get a model structure whose weak equivalences are the rational homology isomorphisms.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 10.2]{Bousfield1975}}]\label{unptedhomlocq} There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{H\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ on the category of simplicial sets in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is a rational homology isomorphism; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent This model category is combinatorial, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Example \ref{defsmcdef1}.
\end{theorem}
\noindent We use the pointed version of Theorem \ref{unptedhomloc}, which holds by the same proof.

\begin{theorem}\label{ptedhomloc} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}$ on the category of pointed simplicial sets in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is an $M$-local homology isomorphism; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent This model category is combinatorial, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Example \ref{defsmcdef2}.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{An alternative description of the family of model structures}\label{section8.2}
\noindent For a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, we show that the model category $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ from Theorem \ref{ididitididit} is the left transfer of the model category $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}$ from Theorem \ref{ptedhomloc} along the adjunction $$F \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \colon E_{1}$$ from Lemma \ref{yesquite}(2). For this left transfer, we rely on Theorem \ref{leftinducetheorem}, so we verify the condition in its hypothesis.

\begin{lemma}\label{verifybhkkrs2} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Let $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ be a map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. If $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to every monomorphism of $1$-reduced simplicial sets, then $f$ is an $M$-local homology isomorphism.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} Suppose $f$ has the right lifting property with respect to every monomorphism of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. By Lemma \ref{verifybhkkrs}, we know that $f$ is a weak homotopy equivalence. Then, Theorem \ref{dkcited} informs us that $f$ is an integral homology isomorphism. Therefore, by Lemma \ref{forlateruselemma}, $f$ is an $M$-local homology isomorphism.
\end{proof}
\noindent We invoke Theorem \ref{leftinducetheorem} to give an alternative approach to the family of model structures from Theorem \ref{ididitididit}.

\begin{proposition}\label{rightinduceeyebrow2} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. The model category $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ is the left transfer of the model category $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}$ along the adjunction $$F \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \colon E_{1}.$$
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Theorem \ref{leftinducetheorem} says that we can left transfer the model structure $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}_{\ast}$ along the adjunction $$F \colon {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1} \rightleftarrows {\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{\ast} \colon E_{1}$$ to a model structure on ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$ in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is an $M$-local homology isomorphism; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent By Theorem \ref{whiteheadserrepair}, a map of $1$-reduced simplicial sets $f$ is an $M$-local homology isomorphism if and only if $f$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence. Thus, the left transfer gives us the model category $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}_{1}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Rational homotopy theory beyond $1$-connected spaces}\label{section9}
\noindent Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. We study some elements of rational homotopy theory beyond $1$-connected spaces in order to extend Quillen's family of model structures from $1$-reduced simplicial sets to all simplicial sets. In particular, we characterize $M$-local spaces in terms of function complexes, which prepares us for the left Bousfield localization in Section \ref{section10}. We expect this characterization to be familiar to the rational homotopy theory community, but we could not find it in the literature, so we provide a self-contained proof.

\subsection{Localization of spaces}\label{section9.1}
\noindent We adopt the generalization of rational homotopy theory to all spaces from \cite[\S 6]{GTHT2000}; in particular, we generalize Definition \ref{defrhtdef} to all spaces as follows.

\begin{definition}\label{defrhtnscdef} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$.

\begin{enumerate}

\item A continuous map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is an \emph{$M$-local homotopy equivalence} if, for every $n \leq 1$, the induced map $$f_{\ast} \colon \pi_{n}(X) \rightarrow \pi_{n}(Y)$$ is an isomorphism and, for every $n \geq 2$, the induced map $$f_{\ast} \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} \operatorname{id}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \colon \pi_{n}(X) \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} M^{-1}\mathbb{Z} \rightarrow \pi_{n}(Y) \underset{\mathbb{Z}}{\otimes} M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$$ is an isomorphism.

\item A space $Y$ is \emph{$M$-local} if, for every $n \geq 2$, the abelian group $\pi_{n}(Y)$ is an $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$-module.

\item An \emph{$M$-localization} of a space $Y$ is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence $f \colon Y \rightarrow Y_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ with $M$-local target.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent When $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$, we speak of a \emph{rational homotopy equivalence}, a \emph{rational space}, and a \emph{rationalization}.

\end{definition}
\noindent Our goal of extending Quillen's family of model structures to all simplicial sets precludes us from imposing any connectedness or nilpotency hypothesis on spaces. The absence of any connectedness hypothesis forces us to keep track of the basepoint across Definition \ref{defrhtnscdef} and approach disconnected spaces component-by-component. The absence of any nilpotency hypothesis deprives us of any sensible notion of tensoring the fundamental group with $\mathbb{Q}$, so we have to ask that a rational homotopy equivalence induce an isomorphism of fundamental groups.

\begin{example}\label{defrhtnscdef1} Every weak homotopy equivalence is an $M$-local homotopy equivalence. Also, every $M$-local homotopy equivalence of $M$-local spaces is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{example}

\begin{example}\label{defrhtnscdef2} The spaces $S^{1}$, $S^{1} \vee S^{1}$, and $\mathbb{R}P^{\infty}$ are $M$-local because their higher homotopy groups vanish.
\end{example}
\noindent Example \ref{defrhtnscdef2} begs the question of why our definition of an $M$-local space imposes no condition on the fundamental group. In particular, we can ask that, for every element $m$ of $M$, the $m$-th power map $g \mapsto g^{m}$ be a self-bijection of the fundamental group, even though it is generally not a homomorphism for non-abelian groups. The problem with imposing this additional condition is that, with this stricter definition, there are spaces that do not admit any $M$-localization. This striking result is proven by Casacuberta in \cite[Proposition 2.1]{Casacuberta1993}. \\ \\
On the contrary, in our generalization of rational homotopy theory, every space can be localized, albeit not with the universal property that the localization of $1$-connected spaces enjoys.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[\S 6]{GTHT2000}}]\label{thebigdealsad} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Every space admits an $M$-localization.
\end{theorem}
\noindent The proof of the theorem is concise and it sheds light on what goes wrong with the universal property of localization, so we present it to provide some commentary afterwards.

\begin{proof} If $X$ is a connected space, then its $1$st Postnikov approximation yields the fibration $$\widetilde{X} \rightarrow X \rightarrow K(\pi_{1}(X), 1),$$ where $\widetilde{X}$ is the universal cover of $X$ and $K(\pi_{1}(X), 1)$ is an Eilenberg-Mac Lane space. Since $\widetilde{X}$ is a $1$-connected space, we invoke fiberwise $M$-localization, in the sense of \cite[Theorem 6.1.3]{Hirschhorn2003}, to obtain a map of fibrations
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\widetilde{X}} \&\& {\widetilde{X}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}} \\
	\\
	X \&\& {X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}} \\
	\\
	\& {K(\pi_{1}(X), 1),}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=5-2]
	\arrow[from=3-3, to=5-2]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where the map on homotopy fibers $\widetilde{X} \rightarrow \widetilde{X}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is an $M$-localization of the $1$-connected space $\widetilde{X}$ and, by the induced map of long exact sequences of homotopy groups, the map on total spaces $X \rightarrow X_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is the required $M$-localization of $X$. Applying this treatment to each component yields the claim for disconnected spaces.
\end{proof}
\noindent The downside with fiberwise localization in our setting is that, because it applies to fibrations over a fixed base space $B$, its universal property states that it is initial among fibrations over $B$ with local fibers. Thus, our application of fiberwise localization in the proof of Theorem \ref{thebigdealsad} only yields a universal property of $M$-localization over the Eilenberg-Mac Lane space $K(\pi_{1}(X), 1)$, and we are neither aware of how to get rid of this restriction nor convinced that we should be able to do so in the first place. \\ \\
The absence of an analog of the universal property of localization for $1$-connected spaces has the following domino effect. The universal property of localization was instrumental for our characterization of local homotopy equivalences in terms of function complexes, which in turn gave us the description of the weak equivalences in the left Bousfield localization in Section \ref{section7}. Thus, the left Bousfield localization in Section \ref{section10} is only a partial extension of that in Section \ref{section7} to all simplicial sets: we produce model structures whose fibrant objects are the local simplicial sets, but we do not know whether the weak equivalences are the local homotopy equivalences.

\subsection{The construction of local spheres}\label{section9.2}
\noindent Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$ and let $n \geq 2$. To salvage our function complex characterization of $M$-local spaces in the absence of a universal property for $M$-localizatrion, we need the details of the CW complex construction of the $M$-local $n$-sphere $S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ from \cite[Chapter 9(a)]{FHT2001}. \\ \\
Let $\mathbb{N}$ denote the set of positive integers. We enumerate
$$M \cap \mathbb{N}=\left\{k_{1}, k_{2}, k_{3}, \dots\right\}.$$
We denote the $(n+1)$-disk by $D^{n+1}$. The $M$-local $n$-sphere $S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is constructed as the pushout
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\coprod\limits_{j=1}^{\infty} (S^{n})_{j}} \&\& {\bigvee\limits_{i=0}^{\infty} (S^{n})_{i}} \\
	\\
	{\coprod\limits_{j=1}^{\infty} (D^{n+1})_{j}} \&\& {S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}},}
	\arrow["h", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where, for $j \geq 1$, the $j$-th $(n+1)$-cell $(D^{n+1})_{j}$ is attached to $\bigvee\limits_{i=0}^{\infty} (S^{n})_{i}$ by an attaching map $h$ given by
$$S^{n} \xrightarrow{\textrm{pinch}} S^{n} \vee S^{n} \xrightarrow{\operatorname{id}_{S^{n}} \vee (-k_{j})} (S^{n})_{j-1} \vee (S^{n})_{j} \xrightarrow{\textrm{inclusion}} \bigvee\limits_{i=0}^{\infty} (S^{n})_{i}.$$
The map $$(-k_{j}) \colon S^{n} \rightarrow (S^{n})_{j}$$
is a self-map of the $n$-sphere $S^{n}$ that has degree $(-k_{j})$. In other words, the $1$-connected CW complex $$S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}=\left(\bigvee\limits_{i=0}^{\infty} (S^{n})_{i}\right) \underset{h}{\bigcup} \left(\coprod\limits_{j=1}^{\infty} (D^{n+1})_{j}\right)$$ is the mapping telescope of the sequence of maps $$S^{n} \xrightarrow{k_{1}} S^{n} \xrightarrow{k_{2}} S^{n} \xrightarrow{k_{3}} \cdots.$$

\subsection{A characterization of local spaces}\label{section9.3}
\noindent Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. We use the construction of $S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ from Section \ref{section9.2}, including our notation, to prove the following key lemma.

\begin{lemma}\label{thebigdealnonsc} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$ and let $n \geq 2$. Let $Y$ be a space such that $\pi_{n}(Y)$ is an $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$-module. Then, every map $f \colon S^{n} \rightarrow Y$ extends uniquely up to homotopy to a map $g \colon S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \rightarrow Y$ such that the diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{S^{n}} \&\& {S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}} \\
	\\
	\&\& {Y}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["g"', dashed, from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent commutes.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof} Given a map $f \colon S^{n} \rightarrow Y$, we know that $[f]$ is an element of the $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}$-module $\pi_{n}(Y)$. Thus, there exists a unique element $[u]$ of $\pi_{n}(Y)$ such that $$[f]=k_{1} \cdot [u],$$ and the representative $u \colon S^{n} \rightarrow Y$ of $[u]$ is unique up to homotopy. Using the notation in our construction of $$S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}=\left(\bigvee\limits_{i=0}^{\infty} (S^{n})_{i}\right) \underset{h}{\bigcup} \left(\coprod\limits_{j=1}^{\infty} (D^{n+1})_{j}\right),$$ we extend $f$ from $S^{n}=(S^{n})_{0}$ to $(S^{n})_{0} \vee (S^{n})_{1}$ uniquely up to homotopy by setting $$g|_{(S^{n})_{1}}=u.$$ As $$[f]-\left(k_{1} \cdot [u]\right)=0 \in \pi_{n}(Y),$$
we can further extend $f$ to $$\left((S^{n})_{0} \vee (S^{n})_{1}\right) \underset{h}{\bigcup} (D^{n+1})_{1}$$ uniquely up to homotopy. Continuing further down the telescope in this fashion yields the claim.
\end{proof}
\noindent In particular, we need the following consequence of Lemma \ref{thebigdealnonsc} to give a characterization of $M$-local spaces.

\begin{corollary}\label{thebigdealnonscbutexplicit} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$ and let $n \geq 2$. Let $Y$ be an $M$-local space. Then, every map $f \colon S^{n} \rightarrow Y$ extends uniquely up to homotopy to a map $g \colon S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \rightarrow Y$ such that the diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{S^{n}} \&\& {S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}} \\
	\\
	\&\& {Y}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-1, to=3-3]
	\arrow["g"', dashed, from=1-3, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent commutes.
\end{corollary}
\noindent We salvage our characterization of $M$-local spaces for the left Bousfield localization in the next section.

\begin{proposition}\label{illdothisonenonsc} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. A space $Y$ is $M$-local if and only if, for every $n \geq 2$, the map $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Y\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}, Y\right)$$ induced by the inclusion $S^{n} \rightarrow S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is a weak homotopy equivalence.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} If $Y$ is $M$-local, then Corollary \ref{thebigdealnonscbutexplicit} implies that, for every $n \geq 2$, the map $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, Y\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(S^{n}, Y\right)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence. The converse implication is established as in the proof of Proposition \ref{illdothisone}.
\end{proof}

\section{The family of model structures on simplicial sets}\label{section10}
\noindent Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. We left Bousfield localize the model category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ of simplicial sets to obtain a model category $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ whose fibrant objects are the $M$-local ones. In particular, we produce a model category $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ whose fibrant objects are the rational simplicial sets. Thus, we partially extend Quillen's family of model structures from $1$-reduced simplicial sets to all simplicial sets. Our family of model structures is meant to axiomatize the generalization of rational homotopy theory that we studied in Section \ref{section9}.

\subsection{The set for the left Bousfield localization}\label{section10.1}
\noindent Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. We left Bousfield localize ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ at the set of maps $$T_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}=\left\{E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}) \rightarrow E_{1}\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}) \mid n \geq 2\right\}$$ from Section \ref{section7.2}. We observe that, by Proposition \ref{comdiagramheqs}, the application of the $1$st Eilenberg subcomplex functor $E_{1}$ is superfluous for this localization, for we are no longer working within the confines of $1$-reduced simplicial sets. In other words, left Bousfield localizing ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ at the set of maps $$B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}=\left\{\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}) \mid n \geq 2\right\}$$ yields the same model category, so we equivalently localize ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ at the set of maps $B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ instead.

\subsection{The family of model structures on simplicial sets}\label{section10.2}
\noindent For a multiplicative subset $M$ of $\mathbb{Z}$, we describe the left Bousfield localization of the model category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ at the set of maps $$B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}=\left\{\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}) \mid n \geq 2\right\}.$$ In particular, we characterize the $B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local objects in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ in terms of the following definition.

\begin{definition}\label{defrhtssetsnscdef} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. A simplicial set $K$ is \emph{$M$-local} if $K$ is a Kan complex and $|K|$ is an $M$-local space. When $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$, we speak of a \emph{rational simplicial set}.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}\label{defrhtssetsnscdef1} By Proposition \ref{comdiagramheqs}, the target of every map in $B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is an $M$-local simplicial set. Note that Example \ref{defkanfibrdef2} says that the target of every map in $B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$ is a Kan complex.
\end{example}
\noindent The following proposition helps us transfer the rational homotopy theory from Section \ref{section9} to simplicial sets.

\begin{proposition}[{\cite[Proposition 1.1.11]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{pshsavemensc} If $A$ and $X$ are simplicial sets and $X$ is a Kan complex, then there is a weak homotopy equivalence $$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}\left(A, X\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(|A|, |X|\right)$$
that is natural in $A$ and in $X$.
\end{proposition}
\noindent By a series of characterizations, we show that a simplicial set is $B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local if and only if it is $M$-local.

\begin{proposition}\label{charthelocalsnsc} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. Let $X$ be a simplicial set. The following statements are equivalent.

\begin{enumerate}

\item The simplicial set $X$ is $B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local.

\item The simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex such that, for every $n \geq 2$, the map

$$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}), X\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}), X\right)$$
is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item The simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex such that, for every $n \geq 2$, the map

$$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|, |X|\right) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|, |X|\right)$$
is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item The simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex such that, for every $n \geq 2$, the map

$$\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, |X|) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(S^{n}, |X|)$$
is a weak homotopy equivalence.

\item The simplicial set $X$ is a Kan complex such that $|X|$ is an $M$-local space.

\item The simplicial set $X$ is $M$-local.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent Hence, $X$ is $B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local if and only if $X$ is $M$-local.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} Firstly, (1) and (2) are equivalent by the definition of a $B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$-local object in ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$. Then, (2) and (3) are equivalent by the commutative diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}), X\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|, |X|\right)} \\
	\\
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}\left(\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}), X\right)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|, |X|\right),}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent whose horizontal maps are weak homotopy equivalences by Proposition \ref{pshsavemensc}. Now, (3) and (4) are equivalent by the commutative diagram

\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}, |X|)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})|, |X|\right)} \\
	\\
	{\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(S^{n}, |X|)} \&\& {\operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}\left(|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})|, |X|\right),}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow[from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow[from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent whose horizontal maps are weak homotopy equivalences by Proposition \ref{comdiagramheqs}. Also, (4) and (5) are equivalent by Proposition \ref{illdothisonenonsc}. Lastly, (5) and (6) are equivalent by the definition of an $M$-local simplicial set.
\end{proof}
\noindent Proposition \ref{charthelocalsnsc} helps us describe the left Bousfield localization of ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ at the set of maps $B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}$.

\begin{theorem}\label{ididitididitnsc} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ on the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ of simplicial sets in which a map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if, for every $M$-local simplicial set $K$, the map $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}(Y, K) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}(X, K)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent A simplicial set $X$ is fibrant in $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ if and only if $X$ is $M$-local. The model category $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ is combinatorial, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Example \ref{defsmcdef1}.
\end{theorem}
\noindent Theorem \ref{ididitididitnsc} partially extends Quillen's family of model structures from $1$-reduced simplicial sets to all simplicial sets. A distinguished model structure in this family is that corresponding to the case $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$.

\begin{theorem}\label{ididitididitqnsc} There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ on the category ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ of simplicial sets in which a map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if, for every rational simplicial set $K$, the map $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}(Y, K) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}(X, K)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a monomorphism.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent A simplicial set $X$ is fibrant in $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ if and only if $X$ is rational. The model category $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ is combinatorial, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Example \ref{defsmcdef1}.
\end{theorem}

\section{The family of model structures on topological spaces}\label{section11}
\noindent Quillen's family of model structures on the category of $1$-reduced simplicial sets finds no counterpart for the category of $1$-connected spaces, for the latter category cannot be endowed with any model structure because it does not have all finite limits and colimits; this failure necessitates the use of $1$-reduced simplicial sets in Quillen's model-categorical approach of rational homotopy theory. \\ \\
To see that the category of $1$-connected spaces does not have all finite limits, consider the pullback square
\[\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=3mm, column sep=3mm, ampersand replacement=\&]
	{S^{1}} \&\& {\mathbb{R}^{2}} \\
	\\
	{|\Delta[0]|} \&\& {\mathbb{R},}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["f"', from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["1", from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}\]
\noindent where $f \colon \mathbb{R}^{2} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is given by the formula $f(x, y)=x^{2}+y^{2}$, so $f^{-1}(1)=S^{1}$ is the unit circle in the plane. In this pullback square, we start with the contractible spaces $|\Delta[0]|$, $\mathbb{R}^{2}$, and $\mathbb{R}$, and we obtain $S^{1}$, which is not a $1$-connected space because $\pi_{1}(S^{1}) \cong \mathbb{Z}$. Thus, we see that connectedness conditions on spaces do not interact well with limits and colimits, in contrast to skeletal conditions on simplicial sets. \\ \\
However, now that we have a family of model structures on the category of simplicial sets, we continue our work by presenting its analog for the category $\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ of topological spaces. This analog is produced by left Bousfield localizing the model structure on $\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$, which we recall in this section. Moreover, because we first characterized local topological spaces and then transferred this characterization to simplicial sets in this section, there is no additional work that we need to do in order to obtain a family of model structures on topological spaces.

\subsection{The model category of topological spaces}\label{section11.1}
\noindent We recall the model structure on $\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ from \cite[\S 8]{DwyerSpalinski1995}. To describe it, we first need to introduce the following notions.

\begin{definition}\label{defauxfortopdef}

\begin{enumerate}

\item Given a sequence of subspace inclusions $$X_{0} \rightarrow X_{1} \rightarrow \cdots$$ such that, for every $n \geq 0$, the pair $(X_{n+1}, X_{n})$ is a relative CW complex, we say that the map $$X_{0} \rightarrow \operatorname{colim}(X_{n})$$ is a \emph{generalized relative CW inclusion}.

\item A \emph{Serre fibration} is a continuous map $p \colon X \rightarrow Y$ that has the right lifting property with respect to the class of inclusions $$\left\{A \times \{0\} \rightarrow A \times [0, 1] \mid A \textrm{ is a CW complex}\right\}.$$
In other words, a Serre fibration is a continuous map that has the homotopy lifting property with respect to every CW complex.

\end{enumerate}

\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{defauxfortopdef1} A continuous map is often said to be a fibration if it has the homotopy lifting property with respect to every space. This notion is stronger than that of a Serre fibration, and it is sometimes called a \emph{Hurewicz fibration} to emphasize this distinction. Every Hurewicz fibration is a Serre fibration; the converse implication finds a pathological counterexample in \cite[\S 1]{Brown1966}, but it holds for maps of CW complexes \cite{Cauty1992}.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}\label{defauxfortopdef2} Every space $X$ is Serre fibrant: the unique map from $X$ to the point is a Serre fibration.
\end{example}
\noindent We are now ready to describe the model category of topological spaces.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Propositions 8.3 and 8.9]{DwyerSpalinski1995}}]\label{modcattopsp} The category ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ of topological spaces has a model structure in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is a weak homotopy equivalence;

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a retract of a generalized relative CW inclusion; and

\item a fibration if and only if $f$ is a Serre fibration.

\end{enumerate}

\end{theorem}
\noindent The model category of topological spaces is simplicial with the following structure.

\begin{proposition}[{\cite[Example 9.1.15]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{topsimpv1} Given two spaces $X$ and $Y$ and a simplicial set $Z$, we define

\begin{enumerate}

\item the function complex $\operatorname{Map}_{{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}}\left(X, Y\right)$ to be the simplicial set with $n$-simplices $\operatorname{Hom}_{{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}}\left(X \times |\Delta[n]|, Y\right)$;

\item the tensor of $X$ with $Z$ to be the space $X \times |Z|$; and

\item the cotensor of $X$ with $Z$ to be the space $X^{|Z|}$ of maps from $|Z|$ to $X$ with the compact-open topology.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent With these definitions, ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ has the structure of a simplicial model category.
\end{proposition}
\noindent The model category of topological spaces is also cofibrantly generated; its generating cofibrations arise from geometrically realizing those for the model category of simplicial sets.

\begin{proposition}[{\cite[Example 11.1.8]{Hirschhorn2003}}]\label{topcofgenv1} The model category ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ is cofibrantly generated.

\begin{enumerate}

\item A generating set of cofibrations for ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ is $$|I|=\left\{|i_{n}| \colon |\partial\Delta[n]| \rightarrow |\Delta[n]| \mid n \geq 0\right\}.$$

\item A generating set of acyclic cofibrations for ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ is $$|J|=\left\{|j_{n, k}| \colon |\Lambda[n, k]| \rightarrow |\Delta[n]| \mid n \geq 1 \textrm{ and } 0 \leq k \leq n\right\}.$$

\end{enumerate}

\end{proposition}
\noindent The model category of topological spaces differs from that of simplicial sets in two noteworthy respects.

\begin{enumerate}

\item It is left proper \cite[Theorem 13.1.10]{DwyerSpalinski1995}, but not by Proposition \ref{leftpropernoworries}, for not every space is cofibrant.

\item It is not combinatorial because the category ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ is not locally presentable \cite[Example 1.18(5)]{AdamekRosicky1994}.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent The latter difference raises the question of whether ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ can be left Bousfield localized. To that end, we remark that ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ shares with ${\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}}$ the feature of being a cellular model category \cite[Proposition 12.1.4]{Hirschhorn2003}. This property is a different way of strengthening the property of being a cofibrantly generated model category, and it allows us to left Bousfield localize ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$, for Theorem \ref{lblexists} holds true if we replace ``combinatorial'' with ``cellular'' throughout \cite[Theorem 4.1.1]{Hirschhorn2003}.

\subsection{The family of model structures on topological spaces}\label{section11.2}
\noindent Our work in Section \ref{section9} yields the promised family of model structures on topological spaces.

\begin{theorem}\label{ididitididitnsct} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ on the category ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ of topological spaces in which a map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if, for every $M$-local space $K$, the map $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(Y, K) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(X, K)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a retract of a generalized relative CW inclusion.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent A space $X$ is fibrant in $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ if and only if $X$ is $M$-local. The model category $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ is cellular, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Proposition \ref{topsimpv1}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof} Proposition \ref{illdothisonenonsc} informs us that $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ is the left Bousfield localization of ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ at the set of maps $$\left\{S^{n} \rightarrow S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}} \mid n \geq 2\right\}.$$
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{ididitididitnsctrmk} By Proposition \ref{comdiagramheqs}, $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ is also the left Bousfield localization of ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ at the set of maps $$|B_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}|=\left\{|\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n})| \rightarrow |\operatorname{Sing}(S^{n}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}})| \mid n \geq 2\right\}.$$ Thus, by \cite[Theorem 3.3.20]{Hirschhorn2003}, the Quillen equivalence $$|-| \colon \mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets} \rightleftarrows \mathcal{T}\mathrm{op} \colon \operatorname{Sing}$$
induces a Quillen equivalence
$$|-| \colon \mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets} \rightleftarrows \mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op} \colon \operatorname{Sing}.$$
This Quillen equivalence captures the fact that the model categories $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ both describe the $M$-local homotopy theory beyond $1$-connected spaces from Section \ref{section9}.
\end{remark}
\noindent Lastly, we distinguish the model category corresponding to the case $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$.

\begin{theorem}\label{ididitididitqnsct} There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ on the category ${\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ of topological spaces in which a map $f \colon X \rightarrow Y$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if, for every rational space $K$, the map $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(Y, K) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(X, K)$$ is a weak homotopy equivalence; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a retract of a generalized relative CW inclusion.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent A space $X$ is fibrant in $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ if and only if $X$ is rational. The model category $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}$ is cellular, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Proposition \ref{topsimpv1}.
\end{theorem}

\section{Rational homology versus rational homotopy}\label{section12}
\noindent We conclude this paper by contrasting Bousfield's localization of spaces with respect to rational homology to our localization of spaces with respect to rational homotopy. Even though rational homology and rational homotopy agree for $1$-connected spaces, this agreement does not extend to all spaces. Therefore, we verify that the two localized model structures on spaces are not equal.

\subsection{Localization of topological spaces with respect to homology}\label{section12.1}
\noindent Our argument is best presented using topological spaces, so we formulate the analog of Theorem \ref{unptedhomloc} for the category $\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ of topological spaces.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 10.2]{Bousfield1975}}]\label{unptedhomloctop} Let $M$ be a multiplicative subset of $\mathbb{Z}$. There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ on the category of topological spaces in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is an $M$-local homology isomorphism; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a retract of a generalized relative CW inclusion.
\end{enumerate}
\noindent This model category is cellular, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Proposition \ref{topsimpv1}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\label{unptedhomloctoprmk} By \cite[\S 2.5]{Bousfield1997}, $\mathcal{L}_{HM^{-1}\mathbb{Z}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ is the left Bousfield localization of $\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ at a single map, so it inherits the properties of being cellular, left proper, and simplicial.
\end{remark}
\noindent When $M^{-1}\mathbb{Z}=\mathbb{Q}$, we get a model structure whose weak equivalences are the rational homology isomorphisms.

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem 10.2]{Bousfield1975}}]\label{unptedhomloctopq} There is a model structure $\mathcal{L}_{H\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ on the category of topological spaces in which a map $f$ is

\begin{enumerate}

\item a weak equivalence if and only if $f$ is a rational homology isomorphism; and

\item a cofibration if and only if $f$ is a retract of a generalized relative CW inclusion.

\end{enumerate}
\noindent This model category is cellular, left proper, and simplicial with the structure from Proposition \ref{topsimpv1}.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Rational homology versus rational homotopy}\label{section12.2}
\noindent We show that Bousfield's localization $\mathcal{L}_{H\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ with respect to rational homology does not coincide with our localization $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ with respect to rational homotopy from Theorem \ref{ididitididitqnsct}. This result stems from the fact that rational homology and rational homotopy cease to agree beyond the realm of $1$-connected spaces, and our proof comprises a particular instance of this phenomenon.

\begin{proposition}\label{theyarenotthesame} The model structures $\mathcal{L}_{H\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ on topological spaces are not equal.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof} We show that the unique map $$f \colon \mathbb{R}P^{2} \rightarrow |\Delta[0]|$$ is a weak equivalence in $\mathcal{L}_{H\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ but not a weak equivalence in $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$. \\ \\
Firstly, $f$ is a weak equivalence in $\mathcal{L}_{H\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ because, by Example \ref{defhlgyisodef2}, $f$ is a rational homology isomorphism. \\ \\
To show that $f$ is not a weak equivalence in $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$, we recall from Example \ref{defrhtnscdef2} that $\mathbb{R}P^{\infty}$ is a rational space because its higher homotopy groups vanish. This recollection renders it sufficient to show that $$f^{\ast} \colon \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(|\Delta[0]|, \mathbb{R}P^{\infty}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Map}_{\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}}(\mathbb{R}P^{2}, \mathbb{R}P^{\infty})$$ is not a weak homotopy equivalence. In particular, it suffices to show that the induced map on path components $$f^{\ast} \colon [|\Delta[0]|, \mathbb{R}P^{\infty}] \rightarrow [\mathbb{R}P^{2}, \mathbb{R}P^{\infty}]$$ is not a bijection. To that end, we find a map from $\mathbb{R}P^{2}$ to $\mathbb{R}P^{\infty}$ that is not null. Such a map is the inclusion $$i \colon \mathbb{R}P^{2} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}P^{\infty},$$ for its induced map on cohomology rings with $\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$-coefficients is the quotient map $$i^{\ast} \colon (\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z})[x] \rightarrow (\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z})[x]/(x^{3}),$$ which is not trivial. Overall, $f$ is a weak equivalence in $\mathcal{L}_{H\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$ but not a weak equivalence in $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{T}\mathrm{op}$.
\end{proof}
\noindent Thanks to Example \ref{defkanfibrdef2} and Theorem \ref{quilleneqsecretly}(2), we can safely apply the singular complex functor throughout our proof of Proposition \ref{theyarenotthesame} to obtain the analogous result for simplicial sets.

\begin{proposition}\label{isaidtheyarenotthesame} The model structures $\mathcal{L}_{H\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathcal{SS}\mathrm{ets}$ on simplicial sets are not equal.
\end{proposition}

