    \paragraph{Causal graph}

    A causal subgraph is formalized as a directed acyclic graph (DAG) $\mathcal{G} = ([p], E)$, where the vertex set \([p] = \{1,2,\dots,p\}\) indexes a collection of random variables \(X = \{X_i\}_{i=1}^p\) with joint density \(f(X)\). The edge set \(E \subseteq [p]\times [p]\) encodes direct causal relationships between variables. For each node \(i\in[p]\), we write \(\mathrm{Pa}_{\mathcal{G}}(i)\) for its set of parents. An edge \(i \to j\in E\) indicates that \(X_i\) exerts a direct causal influence on \(X_j\). 



