    \subsection{Curious Reward of Interventional Exploration for Seeking Causality in Unknown World}
    Accurate identification of the Meta‐Causal Graph cannot rely solely on passive agent experience. First, causal structures inferred from observational trajectories alone may misorient edges. Second, an agent’s experiences cover only a subset of possible world states, leaving portions of the causal graph unobserved. To efficiently explore the state space and discover transitions between different meta states, we design a curiosity-driven reward function:
    % As implied in Proposition~\ref{prop:identifiability_of_causal_subgraphs}, directly learning the causal structure from the observational data cannot guarantee all the edges are correctly oriented. We need to actively explore the system by performing interventions.
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:reward}
        R(\text{do}(X_i^t=x'_i)) = \sum_{i, j\in[p]}H(\hat{M}_{C(X^t)[i,j]}),
    \end{equation}
    where $H$ is the Shannon entropy of the posterior distribution over potential causal relationships given the current state $X^t$. This reward function encourages the agent to explore states in which the underlying causal structure remains uncertain.

    To validate and refine the learned causal structures, we employ interventional verification to directly test whether specific variables causally influence others under different state conditions.
    For each variable $X_i$ in our system, we perform interventions denoted as $\text{do}(X_i=x'_i)$, where we intervene $X_i$ by setting it to a specific value $x'_i$ and observe the consequent changes in other variables. The causal effect of $X_i$ on $X_j$ is then estimated as:
    \begin{equation*}
        \Delta_{ij} = \log P(X_j^{t+1}|X^t, \text{do}(X_i=x'_i))-\log P(X_j^{t+1}|X^t),
    \end{equation*}
    where $\Delta_{ij}$ is the difference in log probability between the intervened and the observational distribution of $X_j$.
    We incorporate the causal effect estimation into the objective function as follows:
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{L}_{\text{mask}}=-\lambda_1\sum_{\{(i,j)|\Delta_{ij}>0\}}\log \hat{M}_{ij}+\lambda_2\sum_{\{(i,j)|\Delta_{ij}<0\}}\log(\hat{M}_{ij}),
    \end{equation*}
    where $\lambda_1$ and $\lambda_2$ are hyperparameters.

