\subsection{Topology of real quotient stacks.}
A distinctive feature of the classical Smith–Thom inequality (\ref{align:inequality-ST}) is its inherently global nature: since varieties are locally contractible, the inequality holds trivially at the local level. In contrast, the inequality proposed in Conjecture \ref{conj:ST} does not seem to be locally trivial. Indeed, any separated Deligne--Mumford stack over $\RR$ is \'etale locally isomorphic to a quotient stack $[X/\Gamma]$, where $X$ is a real algebraic variety and $\Gamma$ is a finite group scheme $\Gamma$ over $\RR$ acting on $X$ over $\RR$, see \cite[Lemma 2.2.2]{abramvistoli-2002} and Lemma \ref{lemma:local-structure} for a refined statement over $\mathbb R$. Even for $[X/\Gamma]$, Conjecture \ref{conj:ST} does not appear to be straightforward. In fact, as it turns out, the topology of a real quotient stack can be quite complicated as the following theorem shows. 

Let $\Gamma$ be a finite group scheme over $\RR$, so that $G$ acts on $\Gamma(\mathbb C)$ via an involution $\sigma \colon \Gamma(\CC) \to \Gamma(\CC)$, and let $X$ be a quasi-projective scheme over $\RR$ on which $\Gamma$ acts. Choose a complete set $H \subset \Gamma(\CC)$ of representative for the non-abelian Galois cohomology group
$$\rm H^1(G,\Gamma(\mathbb C))=\{\gamma\in \Gamma \text{ such that $\gamma\sigma(\gamma)=e$}\}/\sim$$
where $\sim$ is equivalence relation $\gamma \sim \beta \gamma \sigma(\beta)^{-1}$ for $\beta \in \Gamma(\CC)$. Via a twisting procedure, to each $\gamma\in H$ one can associate a finite group scheme $\Gamma_{\gamma}$ over $\mathbb R$, a quasi-projective scheme  $X_{\gamma}$ over $\RR$, and a natural action of the group scheme $\Gamma_{\gamma}$ on the quasi-projective scheme $X_{\gamma}$ over $\RR$ (see Section \ref{sec : twistingdefinition} for details). 

The following is the first main result of the paper. 
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:introlemme-prefere}
Consider the above notation. There is a canonical homeomorphism
    \begin{align*}
    \va{[X/\Gamma](\RR)} \xlongrightarrow{\sim} \coprod_{\gamma \in H} X_\gamma(\RR)/\Gamma_\gamma(\RR).
    \end{align*}
\end{theorem}
We use Theorem \ref{thm:introlemme-prefere} to prove Conjecture \ref{conj:ST} in a number of examples. The following corollary (Examples \ref{ex:exampleabelianvarieties} \& \ref{ex:symmetricproduct}) yields non-trivial cases of stacks of arbitrary dimension, which are not products of lower dimensional stacks, for which Conjecture \ref{conj:ST} holds. % (see Examples \ref{ex:exampleabelianvarieties} and \ref{ex:symmetricproduct})
 \begin{corollary}
         Let $\ca X$ be a Deligne--Mumford stack over $\RR$. Assume that one of the following two conditions holds:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item We have $\ca X = [(X \times X) / \ZZ/2]$ for a real variety $X$, where $\ZZ/2$ acts on $X\times X$ by permuting the factors. 
        \item We have $\ca X = [A/ \langle -1 \rangle]$, where $A$ is an abelian variety over $\RR$ and $-1 \colon A \to A$ the multiplication by $-1$ homomorphism. 
    \end{enumerate}
    Then Conjecture \ref{conj:ST} holds for $\ca X$. 
 \end{corollary}

In addition, by exploiting the local structure of real Deligne--Mumford stacks (see Lemma \ref{lemma:local-structure}), we use Theorem \ref{thm:introlemme-prefere} to prove the following statement: if $\ca X$ is an arbitrary separated Deligne--Mumford stack of finite type over $\RR$ with coarse moduli space $\ca X\rightarrow M$, then the natural morphism on real loci $\va{\ca X(\RR)} \to M(\RR)$ is closed; see Corollary \ref{cor:coarse-map-closed}. 

%has the following consequence regarding the topology of %the morphism $\va{\ca X(\RR)} \to M(\RR)$ induced by 
%the coarse moduli space map $\ca X\to M$ of an arbitrary real Deligne--Mumford stack $\ca X$. 

\subsection{Positive results for stacky curves.}\label{subsec:intro:quotient}
With Theorem \ref{thm:introlemme-prefere} in our hands, we then move to prove the Conjecture \ref{conj:ST} for a large class of stacky curves. This is the content of the second main result of the paper. To state this result, we define a \emph{real curve} as a one-dimensional variety over $\RR$ (see Section \ref{sec:notation}), not necessarily proper. 
\begin{theorem} \label{thm:introST-curves}
Let $X$ be a smooth real curve, and let $\Gamma$ be a finite group scheme over $\RR$ which acts on $X$ over $\RR$. 
Assume that one of the following conditions holds:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The action of $\Gamma$ on $X$ is faithful. 
    \item The group scheme $\Gamma$ is abelian.
\end{enumerate} 
Then Conjecture \ref{conj:ST} holds for the quotient stack $\ca X = [X/\Gamma]$. 
\end{theorem}
The proof of Theorem \ref{thm:introST-curves} is somewhat indirect, as we do not directly compare the topological spaces \( \lvert [X/\Gamma](\mathbb{R}) \rvert \) and \( \lvert \mathcal{I}_{[X/\Gamma]}(\mathbb{C}) \rvert \). Instead, we compute \( h^*(\lvert [X/\Gamma](\mathbb{R}) \rvert) \) and \( h^*(\lvert \mathcal{I}_{[X/\Gamma]}(\mathbb{C}) \rvert) \) separately by combining local and global methods. One of the main ingredients in the argument is the study of the local structure of the natural morphism $\vert [X/\Gamma](\mathbb R)\vert \rightarrow (X/\Gamma)(\mathbb R)$, which we use to analyze the global geometry of $\vert [X/\Gamma](\mathbb R)\vert$. We then compare the two numbers \( h^*(\lvert [X/\Gamma](\mathbb{R}) \rvert) \) and \( h^*(\lvert \mathcal{I}_{[X/\Gamma]}(\mathbb{C}) \rvert) \) using the classical Smith--Thom inequality (\ref{align:inequality-ST}) and a group-theoretic inequality from \cite{AdGF-grup}.

