\subsection{Numerical simulations}
We can quantify $\mathcal{P}_\lambda$ numerically for an attractor $\bar x_i$, \textit{i.e.}, an asymptotically stable equilibrium of \eqref{eq:ODE_det}.
To this aim, we generate random trajectories $x(t;x_0,\eta_\lambda)$ with $x_0 \in B\left(\left\{\bar x_i\right\}\right)$, and assess how frequently they converge to $(\bar x_i - \delta, \bar x_i+\delta)$.

In particular, we perform numerical simulations of the system \eqref{eq:ODE_stoch} using an Euler-Maruyama scheme, with $r=0.1$ and $a(n)=a_{c,1}(n)+0.05$, which guarantees bistability for all the considered choices of $n \in \{2,3,5,8\}$ (\textit{cf.} Fig.~\ref{fig:bif_diag}), and with uniformly spaced initial conditions $x_0 \in B(\{\bar x_3(n)\})=(\bar x_2(n), r+a(n)]$, where $r+a(n)$ is the upper limit of the invariant set for system \eqref{eq:ODE_det} identified in Section~\ref{sec:det:mod}. 

For the various choices of $n$ and for a range of noise intensities $\lambda$, we compute the fraction of trajectories of \eqref{eq:ODE_stoch} that have converged to $(\bar x_3(n) - \delta, \bar x_3(n)+\delta)$ at the time $\hat{t}=100s$ such that the trajectories of the deterministic system \eqref{eq:ODE_det} have already reached their steady state.
We set $\delta = 0.25$, such that $|\bar{x}_3(n) - \bar{x}_2(n)| < \delta$ and $|r+a(n) - \bar{x}_3(n)| < \delta$ for all considered values of $n$. 
Fig.~\ref{fig:sims}a shows $\mathcal{P}_\lambda$ depending on $n$ and $\lambda$: the probability of
converging to a $\delta$-neighbourhood of the attractor is larger if $\lambda$ is smaller (as expected, since $\lambda$ is the noise intensity). If $n$ increases, $\mathcal{P}_\lambda$ first increases and then reduces: more complex oligomers (formed by a larger number $n$ of monomers) initially increase the resilience to noise, but then bring the system to lower levels of asymptotic resilience, linked to the small peaks of $p_s(x)$ for $\bar{x}_3(n)$, observed in Fig. \ref{fig:p_s}.

For a fixed $\lambda=0.01$, Fig.~\ref{fig:sims}b shows $\mathcal{P}_{\lambda}$ for different values of $\delta \in (0,0.6]$, where a smaller $\delta$ corresponds to a stricter requirement. For smaller values of $n$, $\mathcal{P}_\lambda$ is higher for larger $\delta$; conversely, given a fixed probability $\gamma^*$, the smallest $\delta$-neighbourhood of $\bar x_3(n)$ to which convergence occurs with probability at least $\gamma^*$ is smaller when $n$ is larger (more complex oligomers), which is favourable to reduce variability in the concentration of TFs, thus confirming the qualitative observations in \cite{proverbio2022buffering}.

